// Kiln's default workflows, bundled with Slate. Readable copies: mods/slate/docs/KILN.md (3D) and mods/slate/docs/kiln/*.md.
// A game's own edit of a workflow lives at docs/kiln/<id>.md; regenerate this file when a default md changes.
export const KILN_WORKFLOWS=[
{"id":"preproduction","name":"Pre-production","icon":"compass","blurb":"pitch to researched, prototyped, chosen direction","md":"# Kiln: Pre-production\n\nTurns one pitch into a researched, prototyped, chosen direction before anything is built.\n\n### How to use it\n- Fill the builder in Kiln (or tell savi \"pre-production for <pitch>\"). Run sends the prompt, a Planner epic with one card per phase, and a Loom page.\n- Depth: quick = one pass per phase; standard = plus the reviewer; deep = two research cycles plus the reviewer.\n- Record: world.state.prepro[<id>] = fields, phase, sources, competitors, directions, prototypes, pick (vaulted).\n- Why: table tennis's ITTF rules arrived mid-build and forced rework. Rules and real numbers come first.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Intake\n- Read the builder back in plain words; ask only the fields marked Missing, once.\n- Name the project id and the epic \"Pre-production: <pitch>\". prepro.phase = 0.\n- **Gate:** the creator OKs the read-back before any research.\n- Reviewer: every field is in the read-back; each missing one asked once.\n\n## 1. Research\n- **Real rules on:** rulebook first. Cite it for every rule the game touches: serve, scoring, legal play, equipment. (This line shows only when Real rules is on.)\n- Every real-world number (sizes, masses, speeds, timings) FIRST, in a facts table with units and one source per row.\n- Terminology, expert technique, the physics that matter to the feel. 5+ sources (8 on deep) in docs/research/<id>.md and prepro.sources.\n- Reviewer: every number re-checked against its source; an uncited number fails.\n\n## 2. Competitors\n- 4–6 games: best at, missing, controls, retention hooks, store rating, links. A gap map of where this game wins, in prepro.competitors.\n- Reviewer: every link opened; ratings and claims match.\n\n## 3. Refs\n- Real photo, mood, palette and HUD refs to the Gallery with captions, plus the builder's references. Generated images are labelled concept, never fact.\n- Reviewer: every ref has a source or a \"concept\" label.\n\n## 4. Ideas\n- 3–5 divergent directions (core loop, hook, win/lose), scored 1–5 on pillars, feasibility, novelty in a table on the Loom page; prepro.directions.\n- Reviewer: no two directions share both loop and hook.\n\n## 5. Prototypes\n- The top 2–3 as greybox spikes in a scratch place, never the game's own places. Each answers one keep/kill question with a number measured in play; prepro.prototypes.\n- Reviewer: each question has its number; the scratch place goes after Path.\n\n## 6. Path\n- A scorecard of directions, prototype results and risks; recommend one.\n- **Gate:** the creator picks the path (prepro.pick).\n- Reviewer: the pick follows the scores; each risk has a mitigation.\n\n## 7. Brief out\n- design.md: create only if absent, else append \"## Pre-production YYYY-MM-DD\"; never overwrite. Pillars, cited rules, numbers table, core loop.\n- Planner: milestone, epics, first sprint. Loom: the brief page with sources. Each asset to its Kiln workflow.\n- Reviewer: the design.md diff is additive and every rule and number traces to a phase-1 source."},
{"id":"3d-assets","name":"3D assets","icon":"cube","blurb":"sculpted, baked game models through Blender","md":"# Kiln: prompt to Blender pipeline\n\n**Kiln** is how a sentence becomes a finished game model. You describe a thing (\"a velvet reading chair, a little worn\"), and Kiln takes it through concept, design sheets, a sculpted high-poly, a clean game mesh, baked PBR maps, export and placement in your world. Researched against Blender 5.1 and the Blender 5.2 LTS manual; links mark where each step was checked.\n\n**The rule behind it:** no code-box \"potato\" props. Hero pieces are sculpted, then baked onto a light, clean game mesh.\n\n**Pure Blender.** Every mesh is modelled and sculpted by hand in Blender. No AI mesh generators (Tripo, Hunyuan3D, Meshy and the like) at any step. Painted concept art is fine; the geometry is ours.\n\n### How to use it\n- **Just ask Savi for a model.** \"make me a chair for the reading nook\" is enough. With *Savi follows Kiln* on (Slate → Settings → Kiln) she runs these steps without being told.\n- **How deep** (Kiln → *how deep*, read from `world.state.kiln.depth`, default *light*): **off** = Savi builds her normal way and skips this page; **light** = brief, concept, one proof render, checked in game, no crew; **full** = every step below, a wisp crew where it helps, a review at each stage; **extreme** = research every part, modular parts, several candidates per stage judged by a critic, a review after every step. Savi checks it before any asset ask, from chat or from Kiln.\n- **Gates** (marked **Gate** below) are where she stops and shows you something to approve. Turn *pause at gates* off and she keeps going, showing you the results as they land.\n- **Edit this page** to make Kiln yours: change budgets, add a material technique, add a gate. Your edited copy is saved with this game; *reset* brings back Slate's default.\n- A quick prompt shape that works: **what it is · where it stands · size · mood or materials · anything it must do** (open, sit, glow).\n\n### Engine limits (read before any art)\nKiln's **Engine limits** page (`mods/slate/docs/kiln/ENGINE-LIMITS.md`, or the game's own copy at ``) is read before step 0. Its 3D table wins over what Blender can do: **1024² textures (2048² for a close hero, never 4K)**, standard PBR only with every Cycles trick baked, one material where it can be, hero under ~1.2 MB, no Draco / KTX2. When a step below wants more, stop and say so.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Prompt and brief (before any art)\n- **Read the prompt back as a brief:** what it is, where it stands, real size, materials, mood, what it does. Anything missing gets one question, or a sensible default said out loud.\n- Pull the game's own style (its palette and look file, its existing models) so the new piece belongs.\n- **Scope sheet** per room: every item, its real-world size in metres, where it stands (world x/y/z, which surface it sits on), what touches it, whether it moves or animates, and whether a body collides with it.\n- **Budgets.** One pass in frame should total about half a million tris, so plan from that:\n\n  | class | game tris | textures | example |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | big hero furniture | 5–8k | one 1024² set | bed, sofa, wardrobe |\n  | mid prop | 1.5–3k | 512² | table, lamp, crate |\n  | small prop | 300–1.5k | 256–512², or a shared atlas | mug, pot, succulent |\n  | fur, foliage cards | 1–3k | 512² alpha | rug fringe, pothos |\n  | character | 10–20k | 1–2k | NPCs, creatures |\n- **Texel density target:** about 512 px/m for props seen from 1–3 m (first-person interiors). Small props share atlases so the room doesn't load 40 textures.\n  ↳ https://bitsoulhosting.com/marketplace/blog/texel-density-game-assets-texture-resolution-guide\n- **Silhouette features:** the 3–5 shapes that make it read from where the player stands (a lamp's cone shade and thin neck, a chair's wing back and splayed legs). The silhouette gate checks these.\n- **Play size:** how tall it stands on screen in pixels from the usual spot (a 1080p screen, the player's eye at 1.6 m, at the usual distance). That is the size every silhouette check runs at.\n- **Wear (ask it up front, like size):** how lived-in is this? Pick one level: **new** (factory clean), **lived-in** (handled edges, a little dust), **worn** (chips, grime in the crevices, faded where the sun hits), **wrecked** (heavy grime, rust, cracks, missing chunks). Then tick the kinds that apply: dust, grime, edge scratches and chips, rust, moss and lichen, sun fade, water stains, soot. Last, ask: **one look or stages?** Stages means the same mesh ships with one texture set per wear level (e.g. new → worn → wrecked), so the game can age it by swapping sets. Write the answer into the brief as `wear: { level, kinds, stages }`.\n- **Plan the stages in the brief:** name every stage you may want (e.g. lived-in, worn) and, for each, which parts it touches and the mask behind each effect: cavity/AO → grime, convex curvature → chips and polish, world-up → sun fade and dust, height from the feet → moss and damp (e.g. chips on the cap rim only, the knit washes clean). Receipt: `wear: { level, kinds, stages, stageMaps: { <stage>: { base, orm, normal?, glb } } }`.\n- **Model the wear only when the silhouette changes:** a snapped leg, a burnt-through hole, a sagging seat, a missing plank. That damage goes into the high-poly (step 4), or into a separate damaged mesh if it's a stage. Everything else is texture, driven by masks (step 8b).\n- **Family (pick one, write it in the brief and the receipt as `family:`):** trees, humans, creatures, buildings, vehicles, soft goods, hard props. The steps below are the spine every piece walks; the matching **Family** card near the end of this page says what changes in which step, its budget and its own gate. A piece that is two families (a cart with a canvas cover) takes the card of what it mostly is and borrows the other's gate.\n- **Naming:** `<room>-<item>` (e.g. `den-chair`), with `_high`, `_low`, `_cage`, `_collision` for the stages. Texture files are `den-chair_basecolor / _normal / _orm / _emissive`.\n\n## 1. Concept (creator approves it)\n- **→ Slate log: start** as the work begins (ticket in doing, Loom page, brief), and **→ Slate log: concept** once it's painted.\n- Paint the piece (or the whole room, for a room's worth of props) at the camera height the player sees it from, in the game's own palette and style.\n- **Gate:** the creator approves the concept before anything gets modelled.\n\n## 1b. Asset list (from the approved concept)\n- Walk the concept image corner to corner and list **every visible object**, as a table: id, where it sits in the concept, materials, tri budget, wave.\n- Sort each row into **hero model**, **kit** (one model placed many times: books, pots, frames), **shell** (part of the room geometry) or **FX/light**.\n- Waves: the big silhouettes first (W1), dressing second (W2). The list is the build's checklist and the verify lane's contract.\n- One prop alone still gets a one-row list: its budget and wave are what the checks measure.\n- **Kit pieces snap to one grid.** Pieces that join (shelves, panels, wall trims, pipe runs) share a module (e.g. 0.25 m), their origins on grid corners and their ends cut square to it, so placing them is snapping, not nudging. The grid size goes in the list.\n\n## 2. Per-item design sheets (turnarounds)\n**The source rule (never broken).** A turnaround is the *target*, so it is only ever made from:\n- **sourced reference**: real photos or art of the thing or its materials (own or CC0, each link kept), or\n- **new concept art**: front, side, back and ¾ painted *from the approved concept image*, the whole piece or one part at a time.\n\nIt is **never** made from a model: no render, viewport grab, in-game `look`, bake or screenshot of any mesh, ours or an earlier version, goes into a sheet, even as a paint-over base. A sheet traced from the model can only ever match the model, so quality stops rising.\n\n- **→ Slate log: sheets** for every sheet, detail sheet and research picture, with the research links on the asset's Loom page.\n- One sheet per item: **front, side, back and ¾** views, orthographic, plain background, even light, consistent scale, painted with the room concept as reference.\n- **Parts:** a complex piece (a lamp: shade, neck, base; a chair: frame, cushion, feet) gets a **part sheet per part**, each painted from the concept. Parts are modelled and checked on their own, then recombined.\n- **Extra research point.** When a sheet can't show a detail (knit stitch, tufting, a hinge, how fur clumps), paint a **close-up detail sheet** for that piece, and gather real photo references of the material.\n- **Provenance on every sheet:** its receipt says `turnaround.source: \"concept\" | \"reference\"` and lists `turnaround.from[]` (the concept image, the photo links). A sheet with no source, or a source that is a model render, fails the gate.\n- **Sheets are locked once approved.** A revision repaints from the concept or new reference; it never re-derives the sheet from the current model. Model renders are **proofs**: they live in `render[]`, never in `turnaround.sheet`.\n- The sheet is what the model gets checked against at every proof render, the first one and the tenth one. Each revision is measured against the sheet, never against the last model.\n- **Gate (big pieces):** the creator sees the sheets, with their sources, before sculpting starts.\n\n## 3. Blockout (base mesh)\n- Load the approved sheets as **orthographic reference image empties** (front, side, back), aligned to the real size, and model against them.\n  ↳ https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/modeling/empties.html\n- Model to real size (metres, scale applied), origin at the bottom centre, front toward Blender −Y (it exports as glTF +Z).\n- Match the silhouette to the sheet's front and side views first. Proportions beat detail.\n- Check the blockout in the game at its spot before sculpting, so scale problems show up when they're still cheap to fix.\n- **Pivots and sockets now.** Everything that turns gets its pivot where it really hinges (a lid on its hinge line, a drawer at its back, a door on its jamb). Anything the game hangs things on gets an empty named `SOCKET_<what>` (`SOCKET_hand`, `SOCKET_flame`, `SOCKET_seat`) so the game attaches by name, not by guessed offsets.\n\n## 3b. Silhouette gate (nothing gets detail until this passes)\n- **→ Slate log: proof** with each compare sheet and its numbers (again at steps 7, 14).\nMost wasted work is detail sculpted onto the wrong shape, so the blockout earns its detail with numbers, not a glance.\n- Render the blockout as a **flat black matte** (no light, no texture) from the sheet's front and side views, orthographic, and once from the **player's eye** in the room.\n- The sheet side of every compare is the **approved sheet** (step 2), never an earlier model render.\n- Make a clean matte of the sheet's matching views: erase labels, rulers, floor lines and anything the model doesn't include.\n- Score each pair with the scorer in **Appendix: silhouette-gate.py** at the end of this page (written out once to ``): `python3  sheet.png render.png --px <play size> --json view.json --compare compare.png`. It gives the **overlap** (1.0 = the same shape), the **width in 8 bands** bottom to top and the aspect, and writes a compare sheet (amber where the sheet has shape the model lacks, teal where the model has extra).\n- **Pass:** overlap ≥ 0.85 and every band within 0.05 at blockout; ≥ 0.90 and 0.03 when the retopo is checked against the high-poly (step 5).\n- Write misses as **measurements**, not adjectives: \"shade 12% too wide\", \"legs 3 cm short\". Fix, re-render, re-score.\n- **Squint test at play size:** the matte at its on-screen pixel height, beside the player for scale, must still say what it is with every silhouette feature visible.\n- Check the **extreme state** too: the drawer pulled, the lid open, the door swung, the cushion squashed. The shape there is part of the silhouette.\n- **Kits and lineups:** pieces seen together get one lineup render at true relative scale; siblings differ in shape, not only colour.\n- A side the sheet never showed (the back of a wardrobe against a wall) is listed as **inferred**, never claimed as matched.\n- **Proportions come from the measured sheet, never from brief words.** Before the blockout, measure the sheet mattes into `targets.json` (aspect, 8 band widths, key ratios: knee height / body height, limb thickness / body height, part length / total). A brief quotes those numbers; an adjective (\"knees high\", \"slender legs\") never sets a size.\n- **No scorer, no pass.** If the scorer is missing, the step stops until it is restored; \"matches by eye\" is not a gate.\n- **Extreme depth:** three blockouts by three different techniques, side by side; a critic that did not build them picks one by numbers and eye.\n- **Sheets cover every view the scorer and detail need:** front, side, back, top, both 3/4s, and a part sheet for each complex part (legs, head) before modelling starts.\n- **Gate:** the compare sheets and numbers go to the creator for big pieces; small props pass on the numbers.\n  ↳ method adapted from blender-game-skills' gated image-to-3D flow (https://github.com/majidmanzarpour/blender-game-skills)\n\n## 4. High-poly: the right tool for each material\n- **Start from the approved blockout**, duplicated and named `<id>_high`. The blockout stays as the base for the low-poly.\n- **Split into parts** the way the part sheets do (frame, cushion, feet). Each part is its own object, so it can be sculpted, simulated and baked on its own.\n- **Pick the technique per part** from the table below. Research any material not in it before starting.\n- **Hard parts first:** support loops or bevels on every edge that catches light, then a Subdivision Surface (level 2–3). Edges that read sharp in the game get a real bevel, about 1–3 mm, never a knife edge.\n- **Soft parts next:** cloth sim or sculpt. Apply the sim at the frame that matches the sheet, then a Multiresolution modifier for folds, creases and seams.\n- **Detail pass with real scale brushes:** Draw Sharp / Crease for seams, Clay Strips for form, Pinch for hard folds. Medium detail (folds, dents, chips) goes in the sculpt; pores, grain and weave go in the texture or a baked height.\n- **Wear and story:** chips on edges people touch, softened corners on old wood, sag where someone sits. Pick from the brief's age and care.\n- **Proof against the sheet** from front, side and ¾ at the sheet's angles. The high-poly must match the sheet's shapes before retopo starts.\n- **Apply everything:** sims, modifiers, Geometry Nodes ending in Realize Instances. No live modifier goes into the bake.\n- **Gate (big pieces):** the creator sees the high-poly proof beside its sheet.\n  ↳ https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/modeling/modifiers/generate/multiresolution.html · https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/sculpt_paint/sculpting/index.html · https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/physics/cloth/index.html\n\nBox modelling only handles hard goods. Blender's simulation and procedural tools cover the soft stuff:\n\n| material | technique | research |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Pillows, cushions, mattress** | Cloth sim with **pressure** on a subdivided cube, pinned seams, then a multires sculpt for creases and piping | https://www.daz3d.com/blog/back-to-basics-modeling-a-blanket-and-pillow-using-cloth-simulations-in-blender · https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/physics/cloth/settings/shape.html |\n| **Blankets and throws draped over furniture** | Cloth sim dropped onto a collision proxy of the bed or sofa, then apply the frame and sculpt hero folds | https://www.tripo3d.ai/blog/collect/how-to-create-realistic-blankets-using-blender-cloth-physics-kxwbe-lj9om · https://vkgamedev.com/blog/sculpt-clothing-folds-blender-zbrush |\n| **Cable knit, chunky knit** | Geometry Nodes knit stitch along curves, or a knit displacement on the high-poly, then **baked to normal** | https://www.blendernation.com/2024/10/07/creating-a-looping-knitting-effect-in-blender/ · https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P3aNRRswE5Q |\n| **Velvet, channel tufting, buttons** | Multires sculpt with a crease brush along the channels, button pinches as instanced studs | https://vkgamedev.com/blog/multires-modifier-blender-sculpting |\n| **Fur: sheepskin, shag rug** | **Hair curves** with Geometry Nodes fur (Interpolate / Clump / Curl / Frizz nodes), then baked to normal + alpha **fur cards**, with a sculpted lumpy base under them | https://imeshh.com/blog/creating-the-perfect-rug-in-20mins-blender-42 · https://www.blendernation.com/2023/06/05/directing-fur-hairs-with-curves-using-geometry-nodes-tutorial/ · https://yelzkizi.org/create-hair-cards-in-blender/ |\n| **Foliage: pothos, fern** | Sculpt one hero leaf, bake it to a card atlas, scatter cards along curve vines with Geometry Nodes | https://www.blendernation.com/2021/11/15/blender-geometry-nodes-3-0-plant-growth-with-fields/ |\n| **Wicker, macrame, rope** | Curves with a profile, Geometry Nodes weave or twist, then baked | https://bazaar.blendernation.com/listing/twisting-cables-blender-geometry-nodes-tutorial/ |\n| **Hard goods: wood, brass, glass, ceramic** | Subdivision modelling with support loops and bevels. Wood grain and brushed metal go into the texture | |\n| **Wear and damage** | qm tools (edge_wear, cracks, chunks) or an SDF boolean. Cycles-only effects (pointiness, AO wear) must be baked or they won't export | workshop `blender-game-assets` |\n\nEvery tool that makes geometry ends with **Realize Instances** and an applied state before baking.\n\n## 5. Retopology: the clean quad game mesh\n- **Quad-dominant, even edge flow, no ngons.** Triangulate only at export (or let glTF do it). Loops follow silhouette edges and fold lines, so normals stay smooth.\n- Tools: **Quad Remesher** / the QRemeshify add-on for organic shapes, Shrinkwrap + Poly Build for hand-tuned areas, and the base mesh re-used when it's already clean.\n  ↳ https://superrendersfarm.com/article/quad-remesher-blender-retopology · https://garagefarm.net/blog/retopology-a-comprehensive-guide-to-3d-model-optimization\n- Detail smaller than about 1 cm lives in the normal map, not the mesh. Polygons go where the silhouette needs them.\n- **Check:** the tri count is within budget, and the low-poly passes the silhouette gate against the high-poly (overlap ≥ 0.90, bands within 0.03) at play size.\n\n## 6. UVs\n- Seams sit on hidden or hard edges. **Mark sharp on UV seams** (hard edges must be split in UV or the bake will show bands).\n- Uniform texel density, about 8–16 px padding at 1k, straightened shells for wood grain, and mirrored or stacked shells only where nothing unique is lost.\n  ↳ https://studio.blender.org/blog/a-uv-unwrapping-guide/ · https://nastyrodent.com/uv-unwrapping-for-games/\n\n## 7. Baking (Cycles, Selected to Active)\n### Maps explained: normal, bump, height, displacement\n| map | what it holds | where it lives |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Height** (greyscale) | how far each pixel sits up or down: white up, black down | authoring only. Feeds a Bump node or a Displacement modifier |\n| **Bump** (Blender's Bump node) | turns a height map into fake lighting at render time | authoring only. **The game never sees it** |\n| **Normal** (the purple one) | which way each pixel faces, already worked out for the light | **ships in the .glb.** The only surface-detail map glTF and the engine read |\n| **Displacement** | height that really moves the mesh | the high-poly sculpt only. Its shape gets baked into the normal |\n\n- **The rule:** use as much bump, height and displacement as you like while authoring, then **bake it all into one tangent-space normal map**. That one map ships.\n- **Stacking them in Blender:** Image Texture (normal, Non-Color) → **Normal Map** node → the **Bump** node's *Normal* input; the height map goes into Bump's *Height*; Bump's output → Principled BSDF *Normal*. Fine grain (wood pores, fabric weave) rides on top of the baked detail.\n- A Bump or Displacement node left in the final material is **dropped on export with no warning**. Bake it, then remove it.\n  ↳ https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/4.2/render/shader_nodes/vector/bump.html · https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/4.2/render/shader_nodes/vector/normal_map.html · https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/addons/scene_gltf2.html · https://texturemap.app/blog/normal-map-vs-bump-map\n\n### The bake\n- **Maps:** tangent-space **Normal** (OpenGL, which glTF expects), **Ambient Occlusion**, **Base Color** (from the high-poly's materials or vertex paint), **Roughness**, **Metalness**, and **Emission** where it glows. When the brief's wear is above **new**, also bake the **wear masks** (step 8b): curvature, cavity, AO and a world-up mask. They cost one bake and drive every wear level.\n- Use a **cage** (a duplicate of the low-poly pushed out along its normals) or tune Extrusion + Max Ray Distance. Weighted-normal / smooth shading on the low-poly before baking.\n- Every material needs an **active Image Texture node** pointed at the target image, or the bake comes out black. Normal / ORM / roughness images are Non-Color, base colour is sRGB.\n- Bake at 2× resolution and downsample (cleaner edges), then dilate margins (16 px).\n  ↳ https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/render/cycles/baking.html · https://www.strayspark.studio/learn/tutorials/bake-high-poly-to-pbr-maps-blender\n- **Check:** no waviness, skewed details or black blotches in a matcap render of the low-poly carrying the normal map.\n\n### Normal-bake checklist (learned the hard way)\n- **Cage:** never one flat push per piece. Clamp each vertex's push to about half the local edge length on tight curves and smooth it, or use Cycles extrusion with max ray distance about 2× the push. Folds and overhangs are where a flat cage self-intersects or misses.\n- **Miss check after every bake:** un-hit texels, or tangent normals with z < 0.25, go back to flat (0.5, 0.5, 1) with a small blur. Dilate UV islands about 8 px. Never fill gutters with the map's mean colour.\n- **Save normals as lossless PNG,** Non-Color, OpenGL +Y.\n- **Bake selected-to-active per piece,** so a throw never steals the duvet's rays.\n- **Fix geometry before the bake** (lift overlapping cloth, drop fringe cards). Never patch maps afterwards.\n- **No bump left behind:** before export, every material's node tree has no Bump, Displacement or procedural texture feeding *Normal*; only the baked normal through a Normal Map node. Compare a Cycles render with the bump on against the glTF in the game: the detail must match.\n- **Gate:** a live close-up at grazing light on every folded surface. Black or noisy patches mean the bake failed; \"the model landed\" is not a pass.\n\n## 8. PBR texturing\n- **Base colour carries no lighting.** Realistic albedo range is about sRGB 30–240. AO goes in its own map, multiplied lightly at most.\n- Roughness guide: polished brass 0.25–0.35, brushed brass 0.4, varnished oak 0.45, ceramic glaze 0.2, velvet 0.8 with sheen, wool and knit 0.9, glass 0.05 (transmission).\n- Pack **ORM**: R = occlusion, G = roughness, B = metallic. That's what glTF and three.js read.\n  ↳ https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/addons/scene_gltf2.html · https://www.khronos.org/blog/art-pipeline-for-gltf\n- Fabric extras: **sheen** (KHR_materials_sheen) for velvet. Glass: transmission / IOR. Exported from the Principled BSDF.\n  ↳ https://generalistprogrammer.com/tutorials/substance-painter-game-texturing-complete-pbr-workflow\n\n### Values pass (before the texture is done)\n- View the base colour, then a render, in **greyscale**. The silhouette features from step 0 must still separate by light and dark, not only by hue.\n- Keep a clear value step between parts that touch (shade vs lamp body, cushion vs frame); the game's warm lamps and colour grade squash hue far more than value.\n- Albedo stays inside the usual PBR range (no pure black, no pure white) so the game's light has room.\n\n## 8b. Wear and grunge layers (from the brief's wear)\n- **Masks first, baked once** from the high-poly onto the low-poly UVs, all Non-Color greyscale:\n  - **curvature** (convex edges are where hands and knocks chip paint and polish wood smooth),\n  - **cavity / AO** (crevices and corners are where dirt and grime collect),\n  - **world-up** (the normal's Z: dust, snow and moss settle on top faces),\n  - **a large-scale noise** (breaks it up so the wear isn't evenly painted on).\n- **Each wear kind is one layer in the material:** a colour or roughness change, blended in by a mask combination. Edge chips = curvature × noise, showing the under-layer (bare wood under stain, metal under paint). Grime = cavity × noise, darker and rougher. Dust = world-up × cavity-inverse, paler and rougher. Moss = world-up × cavity × noise, green and rough 0.95. Rust = curvature + cavity on metal, orange-brown, rough, metalness 0. Sun fade = world-up, a desaturated base colour.\n- **Wear level = how far each mask is thresholded,** not new paint: lived-in about 15%, worn about 40%, wrecked about 75% coverage. One node group, one level slider.\n- **Bake the layered result down** to the shipped base colour + ORM (+ normal if a chip has depth). No procedural wear survives export: the \"no bump left behind\" check (step 7) covers wear too.\n- **Stages:** bake one texture set per level, named `<asset>_<level>_basecolor` / `_orm`. Same mesh, same UVs. In the game, a stage is a material swap on the model (`model.materials.<slot>.texture`). A silhouette change (the snapped leg) is a separate mesh per stage.\n- **Story check:** wear goes where the asset's life puts it. Polish on the arms someone grips, grime around the feet, a sun-faded top on whatever stands by the window, nothing worn on the underside nobody touches. Evenly sprinkled dirt reads as a filter.\n  ↳ https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/render/cycles/baking.html · https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/render/shader_nodes/input/ao.html\n- **One mesh, one UV set:** a stage is a new base colour + ORM only; share the normal map unless a chip changes the shape. Prove it by re-importing both glbs: tris, verts and a UV checksum must match.\n- **Bake masks on the low mesh at texture size:** AO in Cycles with distance about 10 % of the piece's height; world-up and height as an emit bake of the true normal / position; curvature from the mesh edges plus the normal map's detail. Pointiness only renders: it never bakes or exports.\n- **Drive the breakup with 3D noise from the baked position,** not UV noise, so it doesn't tear at UV seams.\n- **Hold each effect to its part** (chips on the outer 20 % of a rim, fade on up-facing faces only, moss below a set height) and write each mask's coverage % in the run log; chips over ~10 % of a part read as camouflage, not wear.\n- **Swap images on the Image Texture node,** never by file path or a loose name match.\n- **Ship a stage** as a second glb on the same mesh (proven on the toadstool stool), or as `model.materials.<slot>` texture overrides at runtime (not yet proven). Save the stage's ORM as JPEG at 1024: as PNG it doubled the file and pushed the glb over the hero cap.\n  ↳ https://helpx.adobe.com/substance-3d-painter/features/smart-materials-and-masks.html · https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/render/shader_nodes/input/geometry.html (Pointiness: Cycles render only)\n- **Check:** render the stages side by side at the same light. Each level must read as the same object, older. Then view it at play size: if the wear disappears from the usual spot, raise the contrast, don't add more noise.\n\n## 9. LODs\n- **The engine already builds the far levels** from a welded, smooth-shaded mesh. That's why a weld before export matters: split edges stop it collapsing.\n- Authored LODs only for heroes that still read wrong far away: LOD0 100 %, LOD1 ~50 %, LOD2 ~20 % (Decimate Collapse with UVs preserved, or a hand retopo), swapped with `visibleRange` on child rows.\n  ↳ https://bitsoulhosting.com/marketplace/blog/how-to-create-lods-in-blender-for-game-engines · https://extensions.blender.org/add-ons/lod-gen/\n- Interiors are close range, so indoor furniture usually needs LOD0 only, plus the engine's levels.\n\n## 10. Collision\n- **Never the render mesh.** Use a few boxes or convex hulls that cover what a body walks into: the sofa is two boxes (seat, back), the bed is the platform. Small props get no collider.\n  ↳ https://www.pixelwolf.net/blog/collision-mesh-is-not-render-mesh-blender-game-assets-guide/\n- If the room is a procedural shell, its walkable proxies can live in the shell's `collider()` export, and the prop carries `physics: none`.\n\n## 11. Rigging (anything that bends)\n- One deform armature with **Mixamo-style bone names** (what the engine's IK, grounding and clip library resolve), positive scale, no root motion, applied transforms.\n- Weights proven at extreme poses before texturing. Rigify control rigs need a clean deform-only export (the \"game rig tools\" approach).\n  ↳ https://cgdive.itch.io/game-rig-tools · https://lucky3d.fr/auto-rig-pro/doc/ge_export_doc.html\n- For simple props (a hatch hinge, a turntable platter, a lid) use **no bones**: separate named nodes animated in the engine or in code.\n\n## 12. Animation\n- One **Action per clip** (Idle, Walk, Open, Spin…), pushed to NLA tracks, exported with `export_animation_mode=\"NLA_TRACKS\"`. The engine plays clips by name.\n- **Shape keys → morph targets** for soft deformation (a cushion squish, a blanket breathing, faces). Keep them few, since morphs cost memory.\n  ↳ https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/addons/scene_gltf2.html · https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/animation/shape_keys/index.html\n- Ambient motion (a bubbling lamp, fern sway, glow pulse) is cheaper as a **material or shader animation** than as baked keys.\n\n## 13. Export\n- glTF binary (.glb), Y-up, apply modifiers, +Z front (`model: { forward: \"+z\" }`), and only the materials actually used. Images inside: JPEG for base colour, **lossless PNG for normal and ORM** (a JPEG normal map blocks up when the CDN re-encodes it).\n- Target **under about 1.2 MB per hero .glb**, under 300 KB per small prop. The world's boot bar is about 450 KB, so props the first picture doesn't need get spawned after arrival.\n- Compression research: Meshopt / Draco geometry and KTX2 textures save a lot, but the engine reference doesn't list support for them. **Open question for the Spawn team** before relying on them.\n  ↳ https://gltf-transform.dev/ · https://compress-glb.com/blog/draco-vs-meshopt/\n\n## 14. QA proof (before it goes in the game)\n- **Gate:** before it goes in the game, it passes this whole list.\n- Re-import the .glb into an empty scene. Measure size, tris and materials. Render a **proof spin** beside the design sheet. The proof goes in `render[]` and is never reused as a sheet.\n- Run the **glTF Validator** on the .glb: zero errors, warnings read and answered.\n  ↳ https://github.com/KhronosGroup/glTF-Validator\n- **Export sanity:** transforms applied (scale 1, rotation 0), no negative scale, normals face outward, one UV map named for the bake, images power-of-two, base colour sRGB with normal and ORM Non-Color, normal map OpenGL (+Y green up).\n- Checklist: sheet source is concept or reference ✔ · scale ✔ · silhouette (gate numbers) ✔ · engine limits (texture size, materials, file size) ✔ · quads / budget ✔ · no bake artefacts ✔ · texel density ✔ · origin at the feet ✔ · names ✔ · file size ✔.\n\n## 15. Placement and alignment to the concept\n- **Look-dev in the engine, not in Blender.** Spawn has its own lights, tone mapping and colour grade, so a Cycles render signs nothing off. The sign-off picture is a `look` in the running game, from the player's eye at the usual spot, at the place's real hour.\n- In that look: the values still separate, metals read as metal (not grey plastic), emissive parts glow without blowing out, normals hold under the room's lamps, and the piece sits in the same light as its neighbours. A material that only works in Blender goes back to step 8.\n- Map each item's spot from the concept to world coordinates (the camera vantage the concept was painted from gets recreated as a `look` camera).\n- Place it, then compare the look **side by side with the concept** (a look with the concept as reference). Nudge until the composition matches: nothing floating, sunk or clipping walls.\n- Lighting pass after placement: practical lights (lamps, candles, fairy lights) at modest intensity. No bloom blowout.\n- Walk it in the game from every place a player stands to see it: the door, the seat, the far corner. Check it on a phone screen too.\n\n## 16. Delivery and sign-off\n- **→ Slate log: landed**: the in-engine look, the receipt summary, the ticket to test, the diary line.\n- The creator reviews in the game. Notes go back to the right step (shape problem → step 3/4, surface problem → step 7/8, placement → step 15). A note on the *design* goes back to step 1 or 2: the concept or sheet is repainted, then the model follows it.\n- Source files (high-poly .blend, bakes) are kept so a revision never starts from scratch.\n- **Receipt per asset** in `studio/assets/<id>.json` (write it from step 1 and update it at every checkpoint, even when the folder doesn't exist yet: create it). Sync it at every checkpoint so Workbench, Gallery, Loom and Planner fill in as the work goes (Slate log 0a).\n- **Gate: Slate filed.** `slateGate(ctx, \"<id>\")` passes before the asset is called landed. The keys it reads: `design.concept`, `turnaround: { sheet, source, from[] }`, `research[]`, `silhouette: { px, gate, views: [{ view, sheet, overlap, worst: { band, delta }, pass }] }` (the scorer's `--json` writes one view), `render[]`, `materials`, `values: { sheet, pass, note }`, `model: { glb, tris, lods }`, `limits: { tex, maps, mats, glbKB, tris, baked, ok, note }`, `placement: { position, rotation, look }` (look = the in-engine sign-off picture) and `inferred[]`. A missing key shows as \"not yet\": the step didn't run.\n\n---\n\n### Rock-solid: the review at every stage\n- A fresh-eyes reviewer (a wisp that did not build it) checks every gate: brief, concept, sheets with their sources, blockout numbers, high-poly, retopo, bake, texture, export, placement. Savi makes the last pass before the creator sees it.\n- Every stage writes its check and result to the receipt. A stage with no recorded check counts as not run.\n- **Stop signs:** a sheet with no source, a model render in a sheet, a Bump node in the export material, a JPEG normal map, unapplied scale, a bake with black or noisy patches. Any one sends the piece back to its step.\n\n### Extra research triggers (stop and research, don't guess)\n- A material not in the table in step 4 → find Blender docs or a tutorial for it first.\n- A sheet missing a view or detail → paint a detail sheet.\n- A bake artefact that survives one cage fix → read the baking manual section again and search the specific symptom.\n- Anything that moves (rig, cloth, morph) → check the glTF export docs for that feature before building it.\n\n### Things you might not have thought of\n- **Material slots named for the real surface** (`velvet_teal`, `brass_brushed`), so the game can tint or swap them.\n- **Atlasing and shared trim sheets** for small props: fewer textures, fewer draw calls.\n- **Vertex colour for variation** (pillow colours from one mesh and one texture).\n- **Alpha mode:** MASK for fur and leaf cards (cheap, sorts correctly), never BLEND for foliage.\n- **Emissive restraint:** strength ≤ 1.5, so bloom doesn't hurt eyes.\n- **Proof renders kept per item** as the visual changelog.\n- **Licensing:** only own or CC0 references and add-ons.\n\n---\n\n## Asset family cards (pick one at step 0)\n- **Pick the family in the brief** (`family:` on the receipt): trees, humans, creatures, buildings, vehicles, soft goods or hard props. Each card below changes only what it names; every other step runs as written above. The family's own gate is checked before the closing gates, and its numbers go on the receipt.\n\n## Family: trees\n- **Step 0 (brief):** name the species and its age (sapling, young, mature), its real height, and how close the player gets (walk under it, or only see it across the valley). Budget: a young tree 1.5–3k tris at LOD0, a mature hero tree up to 5k; one 1024² atlas holds bark and leaves together (bark on one half, leaf cards on the other). Family's reference is a real photo of the species (CC0 or CC-BY), not a painted concept.\n  ↳ https://www.woodlandtrust.org.uk/trees-woods-and-wildlife/british-trees/a-z-of-british-trees/silver-birch/\n- **Step 3 (blockout):** the trunk and branches are grown, not pushed vertex by vertex: Geometry Nodes curves (Curve to Mesh along branch curves with a radius that tapers) or a sapling/branch script; trunk 4–6 sides at the base, 3 at the twigs. The crown is a silhouette first: its height-to-width and how the branches hang (a silver birch weeps at the tips).\n  ↳ https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/modeling/geometry_nodes/curve/index.html\n- **Step 4–7 (high-poly and bake):** no sculpt. The bark is painted procedurally in Cycles and **baked** to the atlas (pale base, dark lenticel dashes, dark patches near the foot); check the bake numbers, not just the picture: a share of dark pixels above zero proves the marks actually came out.\n- **Step 8 (texturing):** leaves are **alpha cards**: small quads carrying a picture of a leafy spray with a transparent background, Alpha mode **Clip / MASK** (never Blend: blended leaves sort wrong and flicker). 250–400 cards for a young tree; each card faces a different way so the crown has no flat sides.\n  ↳ https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/4.5/addons/import_export/scene_gltf2.html\n- **Step 11 (rig):** no bones. **Wind weight lives in vertex colour**: the Crysis layout: **blue** = how much this point sways with its branch (0 at the root, rising up the trunk, 1 at the twig tips), **green** = a per-branch phase so branches don't move in step, **red** = leaf flutter (0 where a card meets its twig, 1 at its free edge; 0 on bark). A wind material reads it; nothing per tick in a script. Export it as the SECOND colour set (Blender glTF export: Vertex colour = Material, Export all vertex colours on), so it lands in COLOR_1 behind a white COLOR_0: in COLOR_0 the game paints it onto the tree (black trunk, rainbow leaves).\n  ↳ https://developer.nvidia.com/gpugems/gpugems3/part-iii-rendering/chapter-16-vegetation-procedural-animation-and-shading-crysis\n- **Step 9 (LODs):** LOD1 drops half the cards and the twigs; LOD2 is a few crossed cards; a **billboard/impostor only at the far LOD** (past ~60 m), never up close, and the switch is smooth so the swap doesn't pop.\n  ↳ https://docs.speedtree.com/doku.php?id=compiler_billboards\n- **Step 10 (collision):** the trunk only (a capsule or a few boxes up to head height); leaves and twigs never block a walker.\n- **Step 13 (export):** the atlas PNG is saved as 256-colour palette PNG when it pushes the file over budget (a 1024² RGBA leaf atlas made a 1.2 MB file; palette PNG took it to 308 KB with the alpha cut-out intact). Vertex colours exported (Use Vertex Color: Active).\n- **Gate (trees):** under budget tris, one atlas ≤ 1024², leaves alpha-clipped with no grey fringe, wind weights present (COLOR_1 in the .glb: blue 0 at the base, 1 at the tips; COLOR_0 stays white, because the game multiplies COLOR_0 into the paint), glTF Validator 0 errors, and a look in the game where the crown reads as the species from 10 m.\n  ↳ https://github.com/KhronosGroup/glTF-Validator\n\n## Family: humans\n- **Step 0 (brief):** age, build, height in metres, clothes, what they do (walk, sit, talk). Budget: 10–20k tris for the whole body with clothes; the **face and hands get about a third of it** (they are what players look at); one 1024² set, 2048² only for a close hero.\n- **Step 3 (blockout):** start from an **MPFB (MakeHuman for Blender)** base with its dials (age, weight, height, muscle), never a mesh from an AI generator. Measure the height in metres before anything else.\n  ↳ https://static.makehumancommunity.org/mpfb.html\n- **Step 11 (rig):** MPFB's **mixamo** rig: bone names like `mixamorig:Hips`, `Spine`, `RightHand`, which the engine reads for foot grounding, IK, held items and every library clip. Never rename bones; never scale one bone on its own.\n  ↳ https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/animation/armatures/skinning/index.html\n- **Step 8 (clothes):** each garment is its **own skinned child** (shirt, coat, boots), weighted to the same rig, never baked into the body; the body under a garment can be removed to save tris.\n- **Step 2 (sheets):** turnarounds come from the concept or reference photos, never rendered from the model.\n- **Gate (humans):** the body stands its stated height in the game, plays a walk without the skin tearing at knees, elbows or shoulders, and the clothes follow the body with no skin poking through at the extreme pose.\n\n## Family: creatures\n- **Step 0 (brief):** what it is, how big, how it moves (four legs, wings, slither). Budget: at or under the character budget (≤ 20k, most animals 5–12k).\n- **Step 4 (high-poly):** **sculpt** the body in Blender (Dyntopo or multires), then retopo to clean loops around the joints that bend.\n  ↳ https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/sculpt_paint/sculpting/index.html\n- **Step 11 (rig):** its **own rig** built for its body (no human rig forced onto four legs); weights checked at the extreme pose.\n  ↳ https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/animation/armatures/skinning/index.html\n- **Step 12 (animation):** a **keyed gait** (walk cycle as its own action), plus idle; every clip pushed down as NLA tracks so the export carries them all.\n- **Gate (creatures):** under budget, the gait plays in the game without foot slide on flat ground, and the silhouette reads as the animal from the player's usual distance.\n\n## Family: buildings\n- **Step 3 (blockout):** a **modular kit on a grid** (wall, corner, door-wall, window-wall, floor tile at 1, 2 or 4 m), snapped together, so one fix changes every copy.\n  ↳ https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/skyrim-s-modular-approach-to-level-design\n- **Step 8 (texturing):** **trim sheets**: one texture of strips (skirting, sill, beam, cornice) shared by the whole kit, with tiling wall textures; no unique texture per wall.\n  ↳ https://www.beyondextent.com/deep-dives/trimsheets\n- **Step 10 (collision):** door and window holes are cut in the mesh **and** in the collider; a door is at least 1.1 × 2.2 m; a body walks in and is stopped by every wall.\n- **Step 15 (placement):** an interior floor and at least one light per room; the ground under the footprint is flattened, never floated over.\n- **Gate (buildings):** walk in through the door, turn round inside every room and walk out; nothing blocks the doorway, no wall can be walked through.\n\n## Family: vehicles\n- **Step 4 (high-poly):** **hard-surface** modelling: clean panels, bevels on every edge that catches light, panel lines as geometry or a normal bake.\n- **Step 11 (rig):** each wheel is its own object, its **origin (pivot) at the hub centre**, named `wheel_fl`, `wheel_fr`, `wheel_rl`, `wheel_rr` so a script can spin and steer it.\n  ↳ https://docs.unity3d.com/Manual/class-WheelCollider.html\n- **Step 10 (collision):** **convex hulls**: a few simple convex shapes for the body (Mesh → Convex Hull in Blender), never the render mesh.\n  ↳ https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/modeling/meshes/editing/mesh/convex_hull.html\n- **Budget:** a player vehicle 8–15k tris, a parked prop car 3–5k.\n- **Gate (vehicles):** the wheels turn about their own hubs without wobbling, and the hull collider stays inside the visible body by no more than a few centimetres.\n\n## Family: soft goods\n- **Step 4 (high-poly):** cushions, blankets, curtains and bags come from a **cloth simulation** (pin groups hold what's tied down), then are **retopologised** to a clean game mesh; very thin or stiff things (a folded napkin) are sculpted instead.\n  ↳ https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/physics/cloth/index.html\n  ↳ https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/physics/cloth/settings/shape.html\n- **Step 7 (bake):** the folds' fine wrinkles go into the normal map; the silhouette keeps only the big folds.\n- **Budget:** 300–1.5k tris for a cushion or bag, up to 3k for a draped blanket.\n- **Gate (soft goods):** it sits on its surface with no gap and no sinking, and the folds hang the way gravity says from where it's pinned.\n\n## Family: hard props\n- **Step 4–7 (high-poly and bake):** **bevel-and-bake**: bevel every hard edge in the high-poly (Bevel modifier, 2–3 segments), bake the rounded edges onto the low-poly's normal map so a cheap mesh catches light like a real one.\n  ↳ https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/modeling/modifiers/generate/bevel.html\n- **Step 5 (low-poly):** or skip the bake: a small chamfer on the game mesh with **weighted normals** (Weighted Normal modifier, Face Area) so flat faces shade flat and edges stay crisp.\n  ↳ https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/modeling/modifiers/normals/weighted_normal.html\n- **Budget:** small 300–1.5k, mid 1.5–3k, big hero 5–8k (the table in step 0).\n- **Gate (hard props):** no black smudges or wavy shading across flat faces in raking light, edges catch a highlight from 1 m.\n\n## 17. Closing gates (every workflow)\n- **Engine limits:** measure the piece against Kiln's Engine limits page (sizes, formats, file size, budgets) and write `limits: { ok, note }` on the receipt with the numbers. Over a limit: stop and say so; only the creator can waive it.\n- **Fresh-eyes review:** someone who didn't make it (a review wisp, or Savi) looks at it in the game, or listens in place, and writes `review: { pass, by, note }`, worst fault first. Fix and look again until it passes.\n- **In-game proof:** a look in the game at the place's real hour (`placement.look`), or for a sound, it playing from its real source in the game.\n- **Gate: Slate filed.** `slateGate(ctx, id)` passes: Workbench, Planner, Loom and Gallery all show the piece.\n"},
{"id":"2d-assets","name":"2D assets","icon":"sprite","blurb":"sprites, tiles and backdrops","md":"# Kiln: 2D assets\n\nSprites, tiles, characters and backdrops for a flat game (side-scroller or top-down), or a 2D layer in a 3D one.\n\n### How to use it\n- Ask for it plainly: \"a hopping frog enemy for the swamp level, 3 frames\".\n- Prompt shape: **what it is · where it's seen · size on screen · style · what it does (frames)**.\n- Savi paints concepts and style frames (the image model) and hands them in; a crew lane's shell cannot paint, so a lane starts from the concept Savi gives it.\n- Every piece in a set shares one style sheet: make it once, then each new sprite only fills in the prompt shape.\n\n---\n\nEvery picture, sound and decision is filed by the **Slate log** workflow: write the piece's receipt at `studio/assets/<id>.json` with `pipeline` set to this workflow (→ Slate log: start when work begins, update it at each checkpoint, → Slate log: landed when it's in the game). Slate files it into Workbench, Gallery, Loom and Planner at every checkpoint, and the last gate is always **Slate filed** (`slateGate`, Slate log 0a): nothing is done until it passes.\n\nRead Kiln's **Engine limits** page first (`mods/slate/docs/kiln/ENGINE-LIMITS.md`, or the game's own copy at ``). A step that wants more than it allows stops and says so.\n\n## 0. Brief\n- Read the ask back: what it is, which place, its size in metres (and on a phone screen in pixels), the actions it needs (idle, move, hit) and which way it faces.\n- Write the receipt now (`status: \"started\"`, `pipeline: \"2d-assets\"`, the concept Savi handed in under `design.concept`) and start the run log `art/<id>/kiln-run.md`.\n\n## 1. Style sheet (once per set, before any sprite)\n- **One frame:** a single picture of the set in place (the hero, one enemy, a tile, the ground) at real size beside the player. Every later piece is checked against it.\n- **One style sentence** for the whole set, e.g. \"soft low-poly storybook, honey and teal, dark outline-free shapes, light from the upper left\". It goes word for word into every prompt and into the receipt's brief.\n- **One prompt shape** for the whole set (the shape above); only \"what it is\" and \"what it does\" change between pieces.\n  ↳ https://aseprite.com/docs/sprite-sheet/\n- **Palette locked:** pick 6–16 colours (the game's palette file if it has one, here `scripts/lib/data/aesthetic.yml`) and use only those plus their light/dark steps. A new colour needs the creator.\n- **One texel density:** pick the pixels per metre once (the 2D starter uses 32 px a metre; a small prop can use 128–192 px a metre) and draw every piece at it, so nothing looks sharper or blurrier than its neighbour.\n  ↳ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pixel_art_scaling_algorithms\n- **One light direction** across sprites, tiles and backdrops (upper left is the usual).\n- **Gate:** the creator approves the style sheet (frame + sentence + palette).\n\n## 2. Pick the road\n- **Drawn in code (default):** a texture script `scripts/tex-<name>.js` paints every frame on a canvas in the game itself: crisp, recolours by tint or a `?param`, nothing to download, edits re-bake at once.\n- **Painted (only when finished art is the ask):** Savi paints with the image model (`spawn paint \"<prompt shape>, <style sentence>\" --transparent`, the first frame as `--ref` for the rest; `spawn cutout` when a background stays). A lane cannot paint: it asks Savi and waits.\n- Either road, note it on the receipt's `steps.road`.\n\n## 3. Sprite sheet\n- **Fixed cell grid:** every frame the same cell size (e.g. 32×32 or 64×64), laid left to right, one action per row. Never trim cells to different sizes: the sprite would jump.\n  ↳ https://aseprite.com/docs/sprite-sheet/\n- **Power-of-two sheet:** the whole canvas 64, 128, 256 or 512 px a side (a 6-frame row of 64 px cells sits in a 512×64 sheet, two cells spare). Old phones and mip-maps want it; Engine limits caps a texture script at 512² unless it is a sheet.\n  ↳ https://www.codeandweb.com/texturepacker/documentation/texture-settings\n- **Padding 1–2 px** of empty pixels inside every cell edge (and between cells), so filtering never pulls a neighbour's colour in (a thin line at the edge of the sprite = bleeding).\n  ↳ https://bugnet.io/blog/how-to-fix-texture-bleeding-and-seams-in-an-atlas\n- **Pivot at the feet:** the bottom-centre of every cell is where the thing stands (the engine's sprite anchor is the feet). Keep the body in the same spot in every frame so it doesn't slide; a flyer keeps its body still and moves only the wings.\n  ↳ https://docs.unity3d.com/2020.1/Documentation/Manual/SpriteEditor.html\n- **Frame counts stay small:** 2–4 for idle, 4–6 for walk, run or flap, 3–5 for a hit or attack. More frames cost memory and rarely read better at play size.\n  ↳ https://www.aseprite.org/docs/animation/\n- Read a cell with `sprite.uvRect` (`{ x, y, width, height }`, 0–1, origin bottom-left): cell i of n across = `x: i * cell / sheetWidth`, `width: cell / sheetWidth`. Always set `sprite.size` in metres.\n\n## 4. Animation timing\n- **fps per action:** idle 4–8 fps, walk 8–12, run 12–15, wings and flicker 12–24. Hold key poses longer (a blink 1 frame, the open eye 4–6).\n  ↳ https://www.aseprite.org/docs/animation/\n- Step frames in the object's own behavior only when the frame changes (12 writes a second, not 60), or declare the clips with `ctx.atlas({ frameSize, animations, fps })` and play them by name. Loops must close: the last frame leads back into the first.\n- Facing is drawn (`?facing=left` on the same script), never a negative scale.\n\n## 5. Transparency\n- Transparent pixels are fully transparent (alpha 0) and edge pixels are the sprite's own colour, never white or black: a pale or dark ring round the sprite (a halo) means the colour behind the cut-out leaked in.\n  ↳ https://www.ppimage.com/blog/alpha-premultiplication-guide\n- Painted art: cut out, then check the edge on both a black and a white ground. Drawn art: hard-edged shapes on a cleared canvas need nothing more; a soft glow is drawn in the sprite's own colour fading to alpha 0.\n  ↳ https://www.codeandweb.com/texturepacker/documentation/texture-settings\n- A code-drawn sprite (`scripts/tex-*.js`) is alpha-tested by default (`cutout`: pixels under 50 % alpha vanish): a soft glow or feathered edge needs `sprite.cutout: false`.\n  ↳ https://www.ppimage.com/blog/alpha-premultiplication-guide\n- Glow that should light the scene is a small point light beside the sprite (and `sprite.emissive` 0.3–0.6 for bloom), not a bigger blurred picture.\n\n## 6. Pixel art scale\n- Pixel art shows at whole-number scale only (1×, 2×, 3× texels per screen pixel); never 1.5×. Size the sprite in metres so one texel lands on whole screen pixels at the usual camera distance, and keep nearest sampling (texture scripts are nearest by default).\n  ↳ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pixel_art_scaling_algorithms\n- Not pixel art (a soft painted or low-poly look): set `sprite.filter: \"linear\"`, or nearest sampling shows blocky steps up close; the 2 px padding then keeps neighbours from bleeding in.\n  ↳ https://bugnet.io/blog/how-to-fix-texture-bleeding-and-seams-in-an-atlas\n\n## 7. Tiles and backdrops\n- Tiles: seamless on every edge, one size for the whole set, edges and corners drawn (the wang set: 16 corner tiles).\n- Backdrops: 2–3 layers for parallax, the far one lowest contrast.\n\n## 8. Place and animate\n- Sprite size in metres matches the brief; feet sit on the ground line (a flyer: its height above ground in the brief).\n- A test piece stands on the proving ground, `visibleTo: \"editors\"`, id `kiln-proof-<name>`.\n\n## 9. Review against the style sheet\n- Look at it in the running game at play distance, beside the player, and next to the style frame.\n- **At 1× on a phone:** a look with `device: \"phone\"` (390×844) from where a player stands; if that body is elsewhere, a scene look framed from the player's usual distance (2–3 m, fov 70): the silhouette and the action read at that size (~30 px), nothing smears.\n- No halo, no bleeding line, no blur from scaling, frames don't jitter or slide, the loop closes.\n- **Gate:** the creator sees it moving in the game.\n\n## 10. Closing gates (every workflow)\n- **Engine limits:** measure the piece against Kiln's Engine limits page (sizes, formats, file size, budgets) and write `limits: { ok, note }` on the receipt with the numbers. Over a limit: stop and say so; only the creator can waive it.\n- **Fresh-eyes review:** someone who didn't make it (a review wisp, or Savi) looks at it in the game, or listens in place, and writes `review: { pass, by, note }`, worst fault first. Fix and look again until it passes.\n- **In-game proof:** a look in the game at the place's real hour (`placement.look`), or for a sound, it playing from its real source in the game.\n- **Gate: Slate filed.** `slateGate(ctx, id)` passes: Workbench, Planner, Loom and Gallery all show the piece.\n"},
{"id":"in-game-art","name":"In-game art","icon":"frame","blurb":"icons, posters, portraits, paintings","md":"# Kiln: in-game art\n\nPictures that hang in the game: paintings, posters, signs, portraits, icons, badges and item art, on a wall in the world or on the HUD.\n\n### How to use it\n- Ask: \"a painting of the lake for the den wall\" or \"icons for the five crystals, same frame style\".\n- Prompt shape: **what it shows · where it hangs (wall, HUD, page) · its real size in metres or pixels · the set's style sentence · the set it belongs to**.\n- A wall piece is three things: the picture, a frame you can see the depth of, and the room's own light falling on it.\n\n---\n\nEvery picture, sound and decision is filed by the **Slate log** workflow: write the piece's receipt at `studio/assets/<id>.json` with `pipeline` set to this workflow (→ Slate log: start when work begins, update it at each checkpoint, → Slate log: landed when it's in the game). Slate files it into Workbench, Gallery, Loom and Planner at every checkpoint, and the last gate is always **Slate filed** (`slateGate`, Slate log 0a): nothing is done until it passes.\n\nRead Kiln's **Engine limits** page first (`mods/slate/docs/kiln/ENGINE-LIMITS.md`, or the game's own copy at ``). A step that wants more than it allows stops and says so.\n\n## 0. Brief\n- Say what it is, where it hangs and its real size: a wall painting in metres (0.8 x 0.6 m), a HUD icon in pixels (48 px). A 48 px icon and a 2 m poster are different jobs.\n- Find the real spot first: which room, which wall, and that nothing else (a window, a door, a shelf, a roof brace) sits there. Read the home plan, then check the room is actually standing in the game; a plan can name rooms that aren't built yet.\n- Pull the game's palette (`scripts/lib/data/aesthetic.yml`) and any pictures or icons already in the game.\n- Write the receipt now, status `started`, and sync Slate.\n\n## 1. The set: one style sentence, one prompt shape\n- **A set is one set:** one frame, one style sentence, one prompt shape for every piece. Write that sentence down before painting anything (\"oil on canvas, moonlit, cool blues with warm window light, loose impasto, no text\").\n- Icons are a set too: same padding, same line weight, same light direction. People understand an icon from experience, so an icon that could mean two things gets a word beside it in the UI.\n  ↳ https://www.nngroup.com/articles/icon-usability/\n\n## 2. Paint the first piece\n- Paint the hardest piece of the set first with `spawn paint`, the game's own pictures as `--ref`.\n- **No painted words.** Titles, names, prices and labels are drawn by the game (a `ui:` panel or a `vector:` on the frame), never painted into the picture: painted letters smear when the texture shrinks and can't be changed or translated.\n  ↳ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mipmap\n- **Gate:** the creator approves the first piece and its style sentence.\n\n## 3. Prepare the picture\n- **Crop off any painted frame.** Image models often paint a frame into the picture; the frame is built as real geometry in step 4, so cut the picture back to the canvas alone (`magick in.png -crop WxH+X+Y +repage canvas.jpg`) and upload that as its own /cdn image.\n- **Match the aspect to the real opening.** Canvas pixels width ÷ height must equal the frame's inside opening in metres (880 x 630 px = 1.397, opening 0.716 x 0.516 m = 1.388: within 1 %). Crop the picture, never stretch it.\n- **Size:** 1024 px on the longest side at most; 2048 only for a hero piece seen close up; never 4096. Big pictures cost download and GPU memory, and engines cap them for phones anyway.\n  ↳ https://docs.unity3d.com/Manual/class-TextureImporter-type-specific.html\n  ↳ https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/texture-format-support-and-settings-in-unreal-engine\n- **Format:** JPEG (quality ~90) for paintings and photos, PNG only when it needs transparency (icons, cut-out signs). Aim under ~250 KB a picture.\n- Record each /cdn/ address on the receipt (`files`, kind `output`).\n\n## 4. Build the frame\n- **The frame is geometry, never a flat card.** One scripted primitive (`scripts/gen/picture-frame.js`): a moulding profile (back, outer rounded lip, a cove, a slip bead at the sight edge) swept around four mitred corners, 2–4 cm deep and 4–6 cm wide, in a real wood (walnut, oak) with an optional gilt slip. A frame's job is to set the picture apart from the wall and tie it to the room.\n  ↳ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Picture_frame\n- **The picture sits inside the frame,** on a plane as a child of the frame, on the rabbet ledge just behind the slip bead (about 2 cm in from the front face, 1 cm off the back), sized to the opening.\n- **Lit by the room, not glowing.** Standard material (`pbr: true`, roughness ~0.8, metalness 0), never emissive, unless the thing really glows (a neon sign, a screen). A painting that glows in a dark room looks pasted on. Keep a gilt slip at metalness ~0.35 indoors: full metal with nothing to reflect reads black.\n- Physics `none` (a body never needs to stop on a picture) or `static` if it sticks out; frames under ~200 triangles at full detail.\n\n## 5. Hang it\n- **Height:** the picture's centre at ~1.45–1.5 m above that room's floor (the gallery \"57 inch\" rule: average eye level). Higher only over furniture or a fireplace.\n  ↳ https://www.ashanging.com/en_us/help/what-is-the-rule-of-57\n- **Off the wall by 1–2 cm.** The frame's back sits 1–2 cm proud of the wall face. Two surfaces at the same depth flicker through each other (z-fighting), worst at a distance.\n  ↳ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Z-fighting\n- **Find the wall by raycast,** from where a person stands in the room toward the wall: the hit's point and normal give the spot and the facing (the frame faces back along the normal). Check the hit's id is the wall you meant, not a window or a neighbouring room.\n- Face it into the room: the frame's front (−Z) toward the player's spot. Turn it with yaw only; walls are upright.\n- HUD art instead: `<img src=\"/cdn/…\">` in ui.js, at the size it was painted for.\n\n## 6. Look from the player's spot\n- **Look from where a person would stand in that room,** eye 1.6 m above the floor, 2–3 m back: does the picture read, does the frame show its depth, is it level and centred on the wall?\n- **Look at the place's real hour** (a night stays dark) and once with exposure lifted, to see the picture and frame clearly.\n- **Legibility at play distance:** the subject reads at 3 m; any words on it (game-drawn) read at 2 m.\n- A side look at a grazing angle proves the frame has depth and no flicker against the wall.\n- **Gate:** the creator sees the piece (or the whole set) in place and says it stays; until then a proof piece is `visibleTo: \"editors\"`.\n\n## 7. Closing gates (every workflow)\n- **Engine limits:** measure the piece against Kiln's Engine limits page (sizes, formats, file size, budgets) and write `limits: { ok, note }` on the receipt with the numbers. Over a limit: stop and say so; only the creator can waive it.\n- **Fresh-eyes review:** someone who didn't make it (a review wisp, or Savi) looks at it in the game, or listens in place, and writes `review: { pass, by, note }`, worst fault first. Fix and look again until it passes.\n- **In-game proof:** a look in the game at the place's real hour (`placement.look`), or for a sound, it playing from its real source in the game.\n- **Gate: Slate filed.** `slateGate(ctx, id)` passes: Workbench, Planner, Loom and Gallery all show the piece.\n"},
{"id":"textures","name":"Textures","icon":"layers","blurb":"seamless surfaces and painted patterns","md":"# Kiln: textures\n\nSurfaces: wood, stone, fabric, metal, terrain ground. Seamless pictures that tile, or patterns the game paints itself.\n\n### How to use it\n- Ask: \"worn teal enamel for the cabinets\".\n- Prompt shape: **material · age and wear · colour · where it's used · how big it repeats**.\n- Savi picks one of three roads (step 1), makes a swatch you can stand on, and shows you a 2 × 2 tiled check before anything goes on a real wall or floor.\n- Worked example: `art/oak-floor-tile/` (build script, run log) and the swatch `kiln-proof-oak-floor` on the Kiln proving ground.\n\n---\n\nEvery picture, sound and decision is filed by the **Slate log** workflow: write the piece's receipt at `studio/assets/<id>.json` with `pipeline` set to this workflow (→ Slate log: start when work begins, update it at each checkpoint, → Slate log: landed when it's in the game). Slate files it into Workbench, Gallery, Loom and Planner at every checkpoint, and the last gate is always **Slate filed** (`slateGate`, Slate log 0a): nothing is done until it passes.\n\nRead Kiln's **Engine limits** page first (`mods/slate/docs/kiln/ENGINE-LIMITS.md`, or the game's own copy at ``). A step that wants more than it allows stops and says so.\n\n## 0. Brief\n- The material, its wear (**new, lived-in, worn, wrecked**, the same words as the 3D page), the colour from the palette (`scripts/lib/data/aesthetic.yml`), where it's used, and how many metres one repeat covers (a floor board set: 1 m; a brick wall: 1–2 m).\n- A concept or reference picture on the receipt (`design.concept`) so \"does it match\" has an answer.\n- Write the receipt now: `status: \"started\"`, `pipeline: \"textures\"`.\n\n## 1. Pick the road\n- **Named seamless texture** (`cdn/texture-<what>.png`): the quickest road, seconds, one picture the CDN paints from its name. Good for background surfaces; add `pbr: true` and the engine derives normal and roughness from it. You can't control the board width or the wear exactly.\n- **Texture script** (`scripts/tex-<name>.js`): when the game computes the pixels (a grid, a gauge, a pattern that changes with state). Keep canvases small, 512² or less.\n- **Blender procedural bake**: when the surface must match a concept exactly, or needs its own normal and roughness maps (floors you look down at, hero walls, anything in raking light). A node tree made only of maths, baked to flat maps. This page's steps 2–8 are this road.\n- **Gate:** the creator agrees the road (it decides cost: seconds for a named texture vs about an hour of authoring for a bake, the bake itself about a minute on CPU).\n\n## 2. Seamless authoring in Blender\n- One script, `art/<id>/build.py`, holds the whole node tree and the bake; every later change edits it and reruns it (headless: `blender -b --factory-startup --python-exit-code 1 --python art/<id>/build.py`).\n- Make every pattern repeat exactly once across the tile: rows and boards from `fract()` of the UV, and noise sampled on a **4D torus** (u and v turned into two circles, fed to a 4D Noise as vector + W). A plain 3D noise will leave a seam at the edge.\n  ↳ https://b3d.interplanety.org/en/tiling-procedural-textures-in-blender/\n- Bake onto a 1 × 1 m plane with UVs 0–1 filling it: wire each channel into an **Emission** shader and bake type **Emit**, one image per map, margin 0 (a flat plane needs no padding). The image node you bake into must be the active node or it bakes black.\n  ↳ https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/render/cycles/baking.html\n- Practitioners bake procedural tiles the same way: emission bake from a tileable tree on a plane; a texture that only looks right in the viewport is not finished until it's baked.\n  ↳ https://blenderartists.org/t/how-to-bake-a-procedural-texture-in-a-tileable-way/1227037/8\n- Bake the base colour into a **float, Non-Color** image and save it as an sRGB file yourself, so Blender doesn't colour-manage it twice.\n- Make the normal map from a baked **height** in the same script, with neighbours that wrap around the edge (numpy `roll`), so it is seamless too.\n\n## 3. The map set\n- **Base colour:** sRGB **JPEG**, quality ~90. No light or shadow painted in; the engine lights it.\n- **Normal:** tangent space, **OpenGL / green-up (+Y)**, lossless **PNG**. glTF and three.js expect green-up; a DirectX (green-down) map makes every bump look like a dent.\n  ↳ https://github.com/KhronosGroup/glTF/issues/952\n- **ORM:** one lossless PNG, **R = ambient occlusion, G = roughness, B = metalness** (the glTF packing). In this engine set `roughnessMap` and `metalnessMap` both to the ORM file; it reads roughness from green and metalness from blue. There is no AO-map field on a primitive material today, so the red channel waits for models (glTF) or is lightly multiplied into the base colour for deep joints.\n  ↳ https://www.khronos.org/blog/art-pipeline-for-gltf\n- **Height:** only when displacement is real (a terrain or a parallax material uses it); otherwise keep it in the build folder as the source of the normal map and don't ship it.\n- Name them `<id>_basecolor.jpg`, `<id>_normal.png`, `<id>_orm.png`; upload with `spawn upload` and put the three `/cdn/` addresses on the receipt (`kind: \"output\"`).\n\n## 4. Texel density\n- Aim for about **512 pixels per metre** on things you walk past; 1024 px/m only for what you stand on and look down at up close (a floor, a desk top). A 1024² tile covering 1 m is 1024 px/m.\n  ↳ https://docs.cryengine.com/display/SDKDOC2/Texels+Per+Meter\n- Keep density the same on neighbouring surfaces; a sharp floor beside a blurry wall is the thing players notice, not the number itself.\n  ↳ https://rebusfarm.net/blog/texel-density-basics-every-artist-should-know\n- In the engine, `wrap: \"repeat\"` makes `repeat` a density per metre: a 1 m tile is `repeat: [1, 1]`, a 2 m tile `[0.5, 0.5]`, so a big wall and a small one share one scale.\n\n## 5. Trim sheets, atlases and decals (for kits)\n- A **trim sheet** is one texture of horizontal strips (skirting, cornice, door frame, plank edge) that many models map their long thin parts onto. One texture and one material serve a whole kit.\n  ↳ https://3dtexel.com/trim-sheets-texture-atlases-the-game-environment-workflow/\n- Lay out strips at the same texel density as the tiling textures, heights in powers of two (64, 128, 256 px), and 8–16 px of padding between them so mip levels don't bleed.\n- An **atlas** packs small unique props (books, jars, labels) into one sheet so a shelf of them is one material.\n- **Decals** add the one-off marks a tile can't carry: a scuff by a door, a stain under a table, a burn by the hearth. `emit(\"decal\", …)` lasts up to 600 s, so permanent marks are a thin plane with a transparent picture, or baked into a unique map.\n\n## 6. Break the tiling\n- Inside the tile: per-board or per-brick colour shifts, and a large soft noise that covers the whole tile, so no one board stands out as \"the same one again\".\n- Over the tile: a **second scale**, a big low-contrast variation layer much larger than the tile, so the repeat stops lining up in the distance.\n  ↳ https://www.worldofleveldesign.com/categories/ue4/landscape-macro-tiling-variation.php\n- On the model: **vertex tint** (a darker colour near walls and corners, paler down the walkway) and decals from step 5. Tint with `material.color` instead of making a new picture for each colour.\n- Terrain ground: `cdn/texture-terrain-<what>.png` in the place's terrain materials, with a `textureScale`.\n\n## 7. Wear masks for surfaces (from the 3D page's step 8b)\n- Use the same masks and levels as KILN.md step 8b, made in the node tree instead of baked from a high-poly: **cavity** (joints, gaps: where grime packs in), **curvature** (worn edges: where bare wood or bright metal shows), a big **macro noise** (traffic polish, sun fading) and a fine noise to break each mask up.\n- One wear dial (lived-in 0.15, worn 0.4, wrecked 0.75) moves every mask's threshold, so a second wear level is a rerun, not a repaint.\n- Wear changes three maps at once: colour (grime darker, chips paler), roughness (grime rougher, polish smoother) and, for chips, the normal.\n\n## 8. Validate (QA)\n- **Seam check:** shift the base colour by half its size both ways (wrap around) and look at the centre cross. Also measure: the colour jump across the wrapped edge should be about the same as across any interior column (ratio ≈ 1.0; above 1.5 is a seam).\n- **2 × 2 tiled check:** four copies side by side, uploaded as a `render` file on the receipt. Look for a blotch, knot or stripe the eye catches repeating.\n- **Repeat blotch at play distance:** in the game, look at a floor from standing height (eye 1.6 m) across 4–8 m of it.\n- **Normal direction:** in a raking-light proof, bumps must read as raised; joints as sunk. If it looks inside out, flip the green channel.\n- **Engine limits:** 1024² default, 2048² only for a close-up hero, **never 4096²**; base JPEG, normal and ORM PNG; each file under ~1.2 MB.\n- **Colour:** mean colour close to the concept, and it still reads in greyscale.\n- **Grain against the concept:** put the bake beside the concept picture at the same size. Evenly spaced dark rings that close into bullseyes read as a contour map, not oak; break the ring lines with fibre noise, add fine streaks along the board, and make knots brown, not black dots.\n\n## 9. Swatch in the game\n- A 2 × 2 m slab on the Kiln proving ground, on **level ground** (check `heightAt` at all four corners; a slab on a slope floats on one side and sinks under the grass on the other): id `kiln-proof-<id>`, `visibleTo: \"editors\"`, `physics: \"static\"`, y from `ctx.place.terrain.heightAt(x, z)`, `material: { texture, normalMap, roughnessMap, metalnessMap, wrap: \"repeat\", repeat: [1, 1] }`.\n- Look at it from standing height at the place's real hour, and once with an exposure lift when the place is dark (night here is permanent). Upload the look to `placement.look`.\n- **Gate:** the creator approves the swatch, then it goes onto the real surfaces.\n\n## 10. Closing gates (every workflow)\n- **Engine limits:** measure the piece against Kiln's Engine limits page (sizes, formats, file size, budgets) and write `limits: { ok, note }` on the receipt with the numbers. Over a limit: stop and say so; only the creator can waive it.\n- **Fresh-eyes review:** someone who didn't make it (a review wisp, or Savi) looks at it in the game, or listens in place, and writes `review: { pass, by, note }`, worst fault first. Fix and look again until it passes.\n- **In-game proof:** a look in the game at the place's real hour (`placement.look`), or for a sound, it playing from its real source in the game.\n- **Gate: Slate filed.** `slateGate(ctx, id)` passes: Workbench, Planner, Loom and Gallery all show the piece.\n"},
{"id":"music","name":"Music","icon":"note","blurb":"themes, loops and stings","md":"# Kiln: music\n\nThe game's soundtrack: a theme for each place, a loop for a mode, a sting for a win.\n\n### How to use it\n- Ask: \"a slow warm loop for the shop at night\".\n- Prompt shape: **where it plays · the feeling · tempo · instruments · how long, loop or sting**.\n- Every number below is measured with ffmpeg on the box, written on the receipt and in the run log `art/<id>/kiln-run.md`.\n\n---\n\nEvery picture, sound and decision is filed by the **Slate log** workflow: write the piece's receipt at `studio/assets/<id>.json` with `pipeline` set to this workflow (→ Slate log: start when work begins, update it at each checkpoint, → Slate log: landed when it's in the game). Slate files it into Workbench, Gallery, Loom and Planner at every checkpoint, and the last gate is always **Slate filed** (`slateGate`, Slate log 0a): nothing is done until it passes.\n\nRead Kiln's **Engine limits** page first (`mods/slate/docs/kiln/ENGINE-LIMITS.md`, or the game's own copy at ``). A step that wants more than it allows stops and says so.\n\n## 0. Brief\n- Where it plays (which place, which moment) and the feeling in three words (e.g. \"calm, warm, sleepy\").\n- Loop or one-shot. A place's bed is a loop; a win, a level-up or a door opening is a sting of 2–8 s that plays once.\n- Length: a place loop 60–120 s (shorter repeats you notice in ten minutes; longer costs download). A menu-in-world loop 30–60 s.\n  ↳ https://www.departuremusic.com/seamless-music-loop-game/\n- Tempo (BPM) and key if it must sit with other tracks; calm 60–80 BPM, play 90–120, action 130+. Write the BPM in the name so the bar length is known (60 / BPM × 4 = one bar in seconds).\n- Intensity layers: does it need to get busier (calm → danger → fight)? Then plan stems now (step 5), not later.\n  ↳ https://www.gamedeveloper.com/audio/composer-guy-jones-talks-adaptive-music-for-games\n- List every track the game needs at once: places, win, lose, boss. Pick one style family for all of them (`lowpoly-cozy`, `scifi-neon`, …).\n\n## 1. Source the track\n- Generated by name: `/cdn/moodboard-<family>/music-<words>-loop.mp3`. The words ARE the prompt: mood, instrument, tempo, \"loop\". It is made the first time anyone fetches it (`spawn fetch <that path>` on the box; the first call may say \"still making\", call again in a minute).\n- A name keeps its first sound forever. A new take is a new name (`-2`, `-v3`); never expect the same name to change.\n- Or the creator's own file: `spawn upload <file>.mp3` (mp3, wav, ogg, flac) gives its `/cdn/value.<sha>.mp3` address. Ask who owns the rights.\n- Write the source address and the words on the receipt (`files` kind `audio`, `t: \"source\"`).\n\n## 2. Listen and measure the raw take\n- Listen once all the way through: does it match the three words? Any wrong instrument, vocals, a hard ending, a long silent intro?\n- `ffprobe` it: duration, sample rate, bit rate, file size. Generated takes run long (the den theme came back 120 s for a 60–80 s ask), so expect step 3.\n- Picture it: `ffmpeg -i in.mp3 -filter_complex \"showwavespic=s=1600x260,showspectrumpic\"` (waveform + spectrogram stacked). Upload the PNG as the receipt's concept: it is the music's \"concept art\" in the Gallery.\n  ↳ https://ffmpeg.org/ffmpeg-filters.html#showwavespic\n- **Gate:** the creator hears it (a listening post in game, step 7) and approves the direction before the set is made.\n\n## 3. Cut the loop\n- Pick the loop length on a bar line if the tempo is known, else at a quiet moment (lowest 250 ms loudness window) near the wanted length.\n- Cut at a zero crossing, then lay the last 1–2 s past the cut over the first 1–2 s with an equal-power crossfade (sine in, cosine out). The end then flows into the start with no click and no drop.\n  ↳ https://www.departuremusic.com/seamless-music-loop-game/\n- Encode with a LAME header (ffmpeg's libmp3lame writes it): MP3 adds ~1,100 samples of padding at the start and end; players that read the header trim it, players that don't hear a gap at every loop. Check the decoded length equals the cut length to the sample.\n  ↳ https://www.compuphase.com/mp3/mp3loops.htm\n\n## 4. Seam and loudness check\n- Seam: RMS of the last 50 ms and the first 50 ms within 3 dB of each other; the sample jump across the seam no bigger than the 99.9th-percentile sample step inside the track (then it is continuous, not a click). Picture the last 0.5 s + first 0.5 s side by side.\n- Loudness: measure with `ffmpeg -i loop.mp3 -af ebur128=peak=true -f null -` and record **integrated LUFS**, **true peak (dBTP)** and **loudness range (LU)**.\n  ↳ https://ffmpeg.org/ffmpeg-filters.html#ebur128\n- Target for this engine (phones and laptops, like a portable console): **−18 LUFS ±2** integrated, **true peak at or below −1 dBTP**. Home-console titles aim at −24 ±2; ambient music may sit a little under the target.\n  ↳ http://gameaudiopodcast.com/ASWG-R001.pdf\n  ↳ https://ansoaudio.com/2016/07/27/loudness-and-metering-in-game-audio/\n- Fix with a plain gain (`volume=+N dB`) when the peak has headroom; use `loudnorm=I=-18:TP=-1` (two-pass, linear) only when it doesn't, because one-pass loudnorm squashes the dynamics a calm piece needs.\n  ↳ https://dev.to/javidjamae/ffmpeg-loudnorm-filter-ebu-r128-loudness-normalization-guide-15d4\n  ↳ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/EBU_R_128\n- Re-measure after the gain and after the encode; write both numbers on the receipt.\n\n## 5. Stems for intensity layers (only when the brief asks)\n- Each layer (pads, melody, drums) is its own file, exactly the same length to the sample and the same BPM, cut on the same bar.\n- Play them together: `ctx.music.play(ref, { layer: \"drums\", volume: 0 })` for each, in the same tick, so they start in step; raise and lower a layer's `volume` to change intensity. Never stop a layer to quiet it: a restarted layer falls out of step.\n- Generated tracks can't be split into stems: ask for separate named tracks with the same BPM and key in the words, then check their lengths match, or use the creator's own stems.\n\n## 6. Wire it in the engine\n- One jukebox per place everyone hears: `ctx.music.play(ref, { fade: 2.5, volume: 0.7 })` from the place's `sim.js` (or where the mood changes). Calling it again with the same track does nothing, so a mood dial can call it every tick.\n- Between places and moods: `play(newRef, { fade })` crossfades (2–3 s for calm, 0.5 s into a fight). `ctx.music.stop({ fade })` ends it.\n- A sting over the bed: `ctx.music.play(sting, { layer: \"sting\", loop: false })`. Under a voice line: `ctx.music.duck(0.25, { duration: <line seconds> })`.\n- Music rides the Music bus: players set its volume on Spawn's own rail dial. No in-game volume slider.\n- A track one player hears with its own play/pause (a radio, a phone) is a placed `audio:` row with `listeners`, not the jukebox.\n\n## 7. Listening post (proving)\n- A small editors-only box on the Kiln proving ground with a button that plays the track to the presser: `behavior: \"scripts/kiln-proof-play.js\"`, `state.clip` (one) or `state.clips` (a list it cycles), `state.label`, `state.volume`.\n- The creator presses it, listens to two full loops (the seam heard twice) and says yes or which words to change.\n- **Gate:** the creator approves the take and the loop.\n\n## Closing gates (every workflow)\n- **Engine limits:** measure the piece against Kiln's Engine limits page (sizes, formats, file size, budgets) and write `limits: { ok, note }` on the receipt with the numbers. Over a limit: stop and say so; only the creator can waive it.\n- **Fresh-eyes review:** someone who didn't make it (a review wisp, or Savi) looks at it in the game, or listens in place, and writes `review: { pass, by, note }`, worst fault first. Fix and look again until it passes.\n- **In-game proof:** a look in the game at the place's real hour (`placement.look`), or for a sound, it playing from its real source in the game.\n- **Gate: Slate filed.** `slateGate(ctx, id)` passes: Workbench, Planner, Loom and Gallery all show the piece.\n"},
{"id":"sound-effects","name":"Sound effects","icon":"wave","blurb":"every act’s answer, beds and footsteps","md":"# Kiln: sound effects\n\nEvery act's answer: a click, a hit, a pickup chime, footsteps, an ambient bed.\n\n### How to use it\n- Ask: \"a sound for the crusher grinding ore\".\n- Prompt shape: **what makes the sound · what it's made of · how heavy · short or looping**.\n- Say how often it happens: once in a while (a door), often (a pickup), all the time (footsteps). The more often, the more takes and the quieter it sits.\n\n---\n\nEvery picture, sound and decision is filed by the **Slate log** workflow: write the piece's receipt at `studio/assets/<id>.json` with `pipeline` set to this workflow (→ Slate log: start when work begins, update it at each checkpoint, → Slate log: landed when it's in the game). Slate files it into Workbench, Gallery, Loom and Planner at every checkpoint, and the last gate is always **Slate filed** (`slateGate`, Slate log 0a): nothing is done until it passes.\n\nRead Kiln's **Engine limits** page first (`mods/slate/docs/kiln/ENGINE-LIMITS.md`, or the game's own copy at ``). A step that wants more than it allows stops and says so.\n\n## 0. Brief (one line per act)\n- List every act that should make a sound: presses, hits, pickups, landings, footsteps on each surface, the bed of each place. For each, write four things: **the act** (a boot lands), **the material** (honey-oak plank), **the weight** (light, medium, heavy) and **how often it repeats** (once, often, constantly).\n  ↳ https://www.gameaudiolearning.com/knowledgebase/how-to-record-foley-for-games\n- Mark each act **in the world** (heard from a place: footsteps, doors, hits, beds) or **UI** (heard only by the player who pressed: clicks, menu chimes). This decides step 4.\n- Pick the game's style family once (the den is `lowpoly-cozy`) and use it for every name.\n\n## 1. One name per kind of act\n- One sound **file family** per kind of act: one coin, one door, one footstep set per surface. A red coin and a gold coin are the same coin; pitch, volume and timing make the difference, never a new file.\n- Name it by what a microphone would hear: `/cdn/moodboard-<family>/sfx-<thing>-<material>-<act>.mp3`, e.g. `sfx-footstep-oak-plank-1.mp3`. Avoid soft words (warm, muted, low, hum): they cut the top of the sound and it vanishes on a phone speaker.\n- A name keeps its first sound forever. A change gets a new filename (`-v2`); never reuse a name hoping for a different sound.\n- **Gate:** the creator hears the key ones (the main act, the main hit) before the rest are made.\n\n## 2. Variation (so ten in a row don't grate)\n- Things that repeat often (footsteps, hits, pickups) get **3 to 5 takes**, numbered `-1` to `-5`, and each play picks one at random, never the same take twice in a row.\n  ↳ https://echoesofsomewhere.com/2023/08/11/footstep-audio/\n- On top of that, each play nudges **pitch by ±5 to 8 %** (pitch 0.92 to 1.08, about a semitone either way) and **volume by ±2 dB** (volume × 0.8 to 1.25). Stay inside about two semitones: past that it stops sounding like the same thing.\n  ↳ https://splice.com/blog/audio-soundscape-for-video-games/\n- Once-in-a-while sounds (a door, a chest) need one take and a small pitch nudge. A streak can climb the pitch (each coin in a row a little higher).\n\n## 3. Trim and level every take\n- **Leading silence ≤ 20 ms.** Measure the time from the start of the file to the first sound louder than −40 dB (`ffmpeg -i take.mp3 -af silencedetect=n=-40dB:d=0.001 -f null -`: if the first line is `silence_start: 0.000…` the take starts at once; a `silence_end` first gives the gap). More than 20 ms and the sound arrives late, after the act. Cut it.\n  ↳ https://ffmpeg.org/ffmpeg-filters.html\n- **One event per file.** A generated footstep often holds two steps or a long tail: cut it to the single footfall (a footstep is about 0.15 to 0.25 s), with a 30 to 50 ms fade at the end so it doesn't click.\n- **Measure loudness in LUFS** with ffmpeg's `ebur128` filter (`ffmpeg -i take.mp3 -af apad=pad_dur=0.5,ebur128=peak=true -f null -`). For a short one-shot read the **momentary max** (LUFS-M); for a bed read the integrated value (I). Pad short files: under 0.4 s the integrated reading is unreliable.\n  ↳ https://vndev.wiki/Guide:Balancing_a_Game's_Loudness\n- **Peak at or under −1 dBTP** (true peak), so nothing clips after the mp3 is decoded or pitched up.\n  ↳ https://www.airwiggles.com/c/gameaudio/peak-level-and-lufs-for-game-audio\n- **Loudness tiers** (the file at volume 1): UI clicks about −24 LUFS-M · footsteps about −27 LUFS-M · impacts and hits about −18 LUFS-M · ambient beds about −30 LUFS integrated, played at gain ≈ 0.15. Every take in one set within ±2 dB of each other. A take far off (louder, clipped, too short) is dropped, not forced.\n- **File size:** mono mp3, 48 kHz, 96 kbps for one-shots. A footstep take is under 10 KB, a one-shot under 50 KB, a bed loop under 500 KB.\n- A trimmed take is a new file: upload it (`spawn upload`) and use its `/cdn/value.….mp3` address; the original name stays as the source.\n\n## 4. Wire it (the sound fires in the same beat as the act)\n- **The same beat.** The sound is emitted by the code that does the act, in the same tick: the swing in the click handler, the hit at the hit point, the footstep when the foot lands. Never wait for a reply from somewhere else first.\n- **In-the-world sounds** carry a `position` and a `maxDistance`: full volume out to 4 m, fading to silent at `maxDistance`. Footsteps 12 to 15 m, a door 20 m, an impact 25 to 30 m, a bed exactly where its source leaves sight. Near-silent far away is good: the browser's default fade (inverse distance) never quite reaches zero, `maxDistance` does.\n  ↳ https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/PannerNode/distanceModel\n- **UI sounds** have no position: only the player who pressed hears them, not the whole room.\n- One-shots: `ctx.emit(\"playSound\", { clip, position, volume, pitch, maxDistance })`. Overlapping copies are fine for footsteps and hits (`mode: \"overlap\"`); a cry or a voice uses `mode: \"restart\"` so spamming it never gets louder.\n- **Footsteps by surface:** one set per ground (oak plank, stone, grass, gravel), picked by what the foot stands on. One set is a data row (the takes, the level, the pitch range), not a new script.\n  ↳ https://www.gameaudiolearning.com/knowledgebase/how-to-record-foley-for-games\n- **Beds:** an object's `audio` with `loop: true`, gain about 0.15, a `maxDistance` that fits the room. Beds add up: two beds in one spot are twice as loud.\n\n## 5. QA\n- Play each act ten times in a row: every press answers in the same beat, no click at start or end, no two plays sound identical, nothing stacks into noise, beds stay a whisper under everything.\n- Check it beside its neighbours: a footstep never louder than a door, a click never louder than a hit.\n- **Gate:** the creator plays with sound on.\n\n## 6. Closing gates (every workflow)\n- **Engine limits:** measure the piece against Kiln's Engine limits page (sizes, formats, file size, budgets) and write `limits: { ok, note }` on the receipt with the numbers. Over a limit: stop and say so; only the creator can waive it.\n- **Fresh-eyes review:** someone who didn't make it (a review wisp, or Savi) looks at it in the game, or listens in place, and writes `review: { pass, by, note }`, worst fault first. Fix and look again until it passes.\n- **In-game proof:** a look in the game at the place's real hour (`placement.look`), or for a sound, it playing from its real source in the game.\n- **Gate: Slate filed.** `slateGate(ctx, id)` passes: Workbench, Planner, Loom and Gallery all show the piece.\n"},
{"id":"voices","name":"Voices","icon":"voice","blurb":"a cast and their spoken lines","md":"# Kiln: voices\n\nSpoken lines: a shopkeeper's greeting, a narrator, a boss's taunt, a radio call.\n\n### How to use it\n- Ask: \"the courier says thanks when you hand over an order\".\n- Prompt shape: **who says it · their character · the line · the mood · when it plays**.\n- What you get back: a casting sheet, each line as its own clip at matched loudness, a subtitle for every line, a talk cue, and the line playing from the speaker in your game.\n\n---\n\nEvery picture, sound and decision is filed by the **Slate log** workflow: write the piece's receipt at `studio/assets/<id>.json` with `pipeline` set to this workflow (→ Slate log: start when work begins, update it at each checkpoint, → Slate log: landed when it's in the game). Slate files it into Workbench, Gallery, Loom and Planner at every checkpoint, and the last gate is always **Slate filed** (`slateGate`, Slate log 0a): nothing is done until it passes.\n\nRead Kiln's **Engine limits** page first (`mods/slate/docs/kiln/ENGINE-LIMITS.md`, or the game's own copy at ``). A step that wants more than it allows stops and says so.\n\n## 1. Cast\n- One voice identity per character, kept forever: a short name in plain words that says who they are, e.g. `den-keeper-warm-welsh-accented-woman`. Every line that character ever says starts with that same name, so it stays the same person.\n- Write a casting sheet per character: name, age, accent (say the region, e.g. \"soft South Wales\"), energy 1–5, one line of character, and **the line that defines them** (the one you'd use to audition). Put it on the receipt as `cast` and a casting-sheet picture as `design.concept`.\n- Keep the identity name in one place in code (a data file such as `scripts/lib/data/voices.js`), never retyped per line.\n- Say non-English words the way the character would: write them as spoken in the line text (Welsh \"croeso\" stays \"croeso\"), and listen for it in step 5.\n  ↳ https://elevenlabs.io/docs/best-practices/prompting/eleven-v3\n- **Gate:** the creator approves the casting sheet before any line is made.\n\n## 2. Script the lines\n- Each line short: one breath, under about 8 words or 3 seconds, so a passer-by hears it whole.\n- Give each line one delivery tag: `[warmly]`, `[whispers]`, `[cheerfully]`, `[fondly]`, `[sighs]`, `[laughs]`. One tag per line; stacking many makes the read unstable. The tag rides inside a `spawn voice` line (step 3), never in the voice's name.\n  ↳ https://elevenlabs.io/docs/best-practices/prompting/eleven-v3\n- Write each line's subtitle text at the same time, exactly as spoken, with the speaker's name. Subtitles are not optional: players rely on them for noise, phone speakers and hearing.\n  ↳ https://gameaccessibilityguidelines.com/provide-subtitles-for-all-important-speech/\n- A line players hear often (\"thanks!\") gets 2–3 takes with different tags, so it doesn't repeat word for word.\n\n## 3. Make the takes (fresh names)\n- A line is a file name: `/cdn/voice-<identity>-saying-<line-words>.mp3`, e.g. `/cdn/voice-den-keeper-warm-welsh-accented-woman-saying-croeso-come-in-out-of-the-cold.mp3`. Fetching it the first time makes it.\n- Everything before `-saying-` is the voice. Never put a tag or take number there: `…-woman-warmly-saying-…` and `…-woman-cheerfully-saying-…` are two different people. (The den keeper's first run did this, and its cheerful line came out about 25 % higher and 50 % brighter than the other two.)\n- A name keeps its first take forever. To retake, change the words after `-saying-` (add `-take2` at the end); never expect the same name to sound different.\n- Generate all of a character's lines in one sitting and keep a table: line, tag, file name, subtitle.\n- A line that needs its delivery tag, or a long or performed one (several sentences, pauses), goes through Savi's `spawn voice \"[warmly] <line>\" --voice <identity>` instead; it prints the /cdn/ address. A wisp without that command puts it on its `needs savi:` line.\n- Budget: speech is 40 an hour, 150 a day for the game, so plan the lines before generating.\n\n## 4. Measure every take\n- Measure each clip with ffmpeg: length, loudness (integrated LUFS), true peak, and silence before the first word: `ffmpeg -i line.mp3 -af ebur128=peak=true -f null -` and `silencedetect=n=-45dB:d=0.03`.\n- Loudness target for dialogue in this game: **-16 LUFS ±1** (phones and laptops count as portable, where -16 is the recommended level; consoles/TV use -24), every line within **1 LU** of the others.\n  ↳ https://vndev.wiki/Guide:Balancing_a_Game's_Loudness\n- True peak never above **-1 dB**; music, effects and voice stack and clip if each sits at 0.\n  ↳ https://vndev.wiki/Guide:Balancing_a_Game's_Loudness\n- Trim silence before the first word to under **0.05 s** (keep a few ms so it doesn't click) so the line answers the press at once.\n- Loudness-match with `loudnorm=I=-16:TP=-1.5:LRA=7`, re-encode mono MP3 ~96 kbps, measure again and upload the matched clip (`spawn upload`); the game plays the matched upload, the receipt keeps both.\n- File size: a 3-second mono line at 96 kbps is about 35 KB; anything over 100 KB for a short line is the wrong format.\n\n## 5. Same person, every line\n- Listen to all the lines back to back: same voice, same accent, same age. One that sounds like someone else is retaken under a new name.\n- Back it with numbers: median pitch per line (python numpy autocorrelation, or aubio) within about **±15 %** of the cast's median, and spectral centroid (brightness) within about **±15 %**.\n- Measure pitch on the voiced frames only (YIN or autocorrelation, frames above 10 % of peak), and let someone else re-run it; a short line's pitch swings with how it's said, so numbers can flag a stranger but only ears pass a line.\n- Write the numbers in the run log and on the receipt's `steps`.\n\n## 6. Wire it: speaker, subtitle, talk cue\n- Play from the speaker: `ctx.emit(\"playSound\", { clip, position, bus: \"Voice\", mode: \"restart\", maxDistance: 18 })` at the character's head. `restart` means a new line replaces the last; a line never stacks on another.\n- Show the subtitle while it plays: speaker name plus the line, solid background plate, large clear font, high contrast; on a HUD or on the speaker's own panel. Name the speaker whenever the speaker changes.\n  ↳ https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/xbox/accessibility/xbox-accessibility-guidelines/104\n- Subtitle options worth offering in a bigger game: on/off, speaker names, size, solid background.\n  ↳ https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/gaming/game-publishing/concepts/metadata-accessibility\n- Talk cue: drive the mouth, jaw or a bounce from the line's loudness envelope (10 values a second, 0–1, measured once with ffmpeg and stored beside the line), or from its length if there's no envelope. Mouth open on loud, closed in pauses. It's the cheap classic; good enough for a small or stylised character, not a close-up face.\n  ↳ https://lipsync.com/blog/lip-sync-gaming\n- Duck the music under speech: `ctx.music.duck(0.35, { duration: lineSeconds + 0.3 })` (about -9 dB). Tools like Wwise duck a whole bus by a few dB with short fades; for words over music, go deeper rather than shallower.\n  ↳ https://gameaudioresource.com/2019/08/24/chapter-15-a-adviser-map-intro/\n\n## 7. Check in place\n- Stand where a player stands and press it: words clear over the music, subtitle readable at night, talk cue moves with the words, nothing repeats too soon.\n- **Gate:** the creator hears the lines in the game.\n\n## 8. Closing gates (every workflow)\n- **Engine limits:** measure the piece against Kiln's Engine limits page (sizes, formats, file size, budgets) and write `limits: { ok, note }` on the receipt with the numbers. Over a limit: stop and say so; only the creator can waive it.\n- **Fresh-eyes review:** someone who didn't make it (a review wisp, or Savi) looks at it in the game, or listens in place, and writes `review: { pass, by, note }`, worst fault first. Fix and look again until it passes.\n- **In-game proof:** a look in the game at the place's real hour (`placement.look`), or for a sound, it playing from its real source in the game.\n- **Gate: Slate filed.** `slateGate(ctx, id)` passes: Workbench, Planner, Loom and Gallery all show the piece.\n"},
{"id":"slate-log","name":"Slate log","icon":"book","blurb":"tickets, Loom pages and gallery, filed as work lands","md":"# Kiln: Slate log\n\nKeeps Slate filled in as work happens: Planner tickets, Loom pages with their links and pictures, and the Gallery. Other workflows call this one at their checkpoints. The creator never has to file anything by hand.\n\n### How to use it\n- Nothing to ask: it runs on its own whenever a workflow says **→ Slate log**, and when any piece of work lands.\n- Ask it directly to catch up: \"log what we did today into Slate\".\n- To see it working: open Workbench (or Gallery, Loom, Planner, Kiln, Studio or the home screen). Slate re-files every receipt when one of those opens, at most once every 15 seconds; ↻ sync in Workbench does it at once.\n\n---\n\nRead Kiln's **Engine limits** page first. This workflow writes only world state and the receipt file `studio/assets/<id>.json`, never the game's own scripts.\n\n## 0a. The receipt files it for you (every workflow)\nEvery workflow, 3D, 2D, in-game art, textures, music, sound effects, voices and your own, writes **one receipt** per piece at `studio/assets/<id>.json`, and updates it at each checkpoint. Slate reads the receipts and files them itself whenever Workbench, Studio, Gallery, Loom, Planner, Kiln or the home screen opens (at most every 15 s), or ↻ sync in Workbench:\n- **Workbench**: the receipt is the asset's page: design, turnaround, research, silhouette, renders, materials, values, final model, engine limits, placement, review and every step's pass/fail.\n- **Gallery**: every picture in it, tagged by kind and grouped under the asset id.\n- **Loom**: one page per piece in **Assets** (brief, status, files and sounds as links, research links with what each was used for, what's still open).\n- **Planner**: one ticket `c-asset-<id>`, `doing` while it's being made, `test` once `status` is `landed`, `placed`, `in-game` or `shipped`. A ticket the creator moved stays where they put it.\n\nReceipt shape: `{ id, title, pipeline, status, room, brief, design: { concept }, files: [{ url, kind, t }], research: [{ title, url, usedFor }], steps: { <step>: \"pass\" | \"fail: why\" | \"skipped: why\" }, limits: { ok, note }, review: { pass, by, note }, placement: { id, place, position, look }, open: [text], log }`; a 3D piece adds `turnaround`, `render: [url]`, `model: { glb, tris }`, `materials`, `silhouette`, `values`. `pipeline` is the workflow id (`3d-assets`, `textures`, `music`, `sound-effects`, `voices`, `2d-assets`, `in-game-art`, or yours). `files[].kind`: `concept`, `drawing`, `reference`, `render`, `output`, `photo` go to the Gallery; `audio`, `video`, `doc`, `model` go on the Loom page as links. `status` runs `started → concept → … → landed`.\n\n**As it works, not just at the end.** Every **→ Slate log** checkpoint means: update the receipt, then file it straight away in the same run_script: `(await import(\"mods/slate/lib/studio.js\")).syncStudio(ctx)`. The creator watches Workbench, Gallery, Loom and Planner fill in step by step.\n\n**Gate: Slate filed (the last gate of every workflow).** Before a piece is called done, run `(await import(\"mods/slate/lib/studio.js\")).slateGate(ctx, \"<id>\")`. It re-files, then checks the Workbench entry, the Planner ticket (in `test` or `done`), the Loom page, the Gallery pictures, `status: landed`, `pipeline`, the concept, materials on a 3D piece, and the three closing gates: `limits` (ok, or waived in the creator's words), `review` (passed, reviewer named) and the in-game proof (`placement.look`, or for a sound an audio file plus its placement). `{ pass: false, missing: [...] }` means fix each line and run it again; the piece isn't landed until it passes. Say the result in one line.\n\nSo **→ Slate log** at any checkpoint means: update the receipt and sync. The sections below are for what a receipt doesn't carry (follow-up tickets, diary lines, links between things, Rules and Journal) and for checking that the filing worked.\n\n## 0. Rules for every write\n- **Never overwrite the creator's words.** Add to a note or a page body; never replace a title, note or page they wrote.\n- **One thing, one record.** Before adding, look for the existing ticket, page or picture (same title, same asset id, same /cdn/ address) and update that instead. The receipt's id is the one name the piece has everywhere: lower-case words joined by dashes (`toadstool-stool`), never changed once filed.\n  ↳ https://blog.cg-wire.com/7-tips-for-better-use-of-kitsu/\n- **One status word list, the same everywhere.** `started`, `concept`, then the workflow's step names, then `landed`. Each word means one thing to everyone; a new word gets added to the workflow page first, not invented on a receipt.\n  ↳ https://blog.cg-wire.com/7-tips-for-better-use-of-kitsu/\n- **Write through world state** in one run_script: `world.state.plan` (cards, shots, links, diary) and `world.state.loom` (chapters, pages). Copy, change, assign back whole.\n- Every record carries `by` and `at` (ctx.now()) so the creator sees what was filed for them. Receipt filing signs as `Kiln`; hand-filed records sign as `Savi`.\n\n## 1. Planner tickets\n- **A new ask** gets a card when the work starts: `{ id, t, col: \"doing\", tag, note, by: \"Savi\" }`. Tags: bug, feature, polish, balance, lore, note. A Kiln piece gets its card from the receipt (`c-asset-<id>`); don't make a second one.\n- **At each checkpoint** add one dated line to the card's note: what stands now, where to look. Like a review note in a studio tracker: what was looked at, what's wrong, what changes next.\n  ↳ https://prism-pipeline.com/docs/latest/plugins/Shotgrid/\n- **When it lands** the card moves to `test` with a note on how to check it. Only the creator moves it to `done`. Columns are the workflow's stages, left to right; a card only moves forward when its stage really finished.\n  ↳ https://www.atlassian.com/agile/kanban/boards\n- **Found along the way** (a bug, a follow-up, a question for the creator): its own card in `todo`, linked to the parent ticket (`related`).\n- The card's `shot` is the best proof picture: the in-game look once there is one, a render before that.\n\n## 2. Loom: pages, links and references\n- **The piece's page.** An asset or feature gets one Loom page in the matching chapter (items, places, characters, mechanics, or an **Assets** chapter it creates if none fits). The body holds the brief, the decisions with dates, and the research links as a list.\n- **Research links** go in the body as `- [what it is](url)` so they click through, one per source, with a few words on what was taken from it (`usedFor` on the receipt).\n- **Pictures on the page:** the approved concept is the page's `img`, and every other picture goes in the body as `![kind](url)`.\n- **Sounds, models, videos and docs** go on the page as links (`files` kinds `audio`, `model`, `video`, `doc`), never in the Gallery.\n- **Keep what it came from.** Each output names what it was made from (the concept, the sheet, the earlier version), so anyone can trace it back; the receipt's `turnaround.from` and the step notes carry this.\n  ↳ https://www.gamedeveloper.com/programming/book-excerpt-i-the-game-asset-pipeline-i-managing-asset-processing\n- **Links between things** in `plan.links` (`{ id, a: \"p:<page>\", b: \"c:<card>\", rel }`): page ↔ ticket `related`, asset ↔ the place it stands `lives in`, part ↔ whole `part of`, blocker `blocks`.\n- **Diary line** in `plan.diary` when something lands: one plain sentence, dated.\n\n## 3. Gallery\n- **Capture at every stage, not only the end.** Each checkpoint adds its picture to the receipt: the concept, the sheets, a proof render, the in-game look. A studio tracker keeps every published version with its picture, so you can see how it got here.\n  ↳ https://outfit7.com/blog/tech/outfit7s-art-pipeline\n- **Pick the right kind** for each picture (`files[].kind`): `concept` painted concept art, `drawing` turnarounds and design sheets, `reference` outside research pictures, `render` proof renders and bakes, `output` the finished thing in the game, `photo` a Slate camera shot. The Gallery's filter chips are these six kinds; anything else files as Photos.\n- **Name each picture** in `t` as `<Title> · <what it shows>`: \"Toadstool stool · proof 2\", \"Toadstool stool · in game\". Short, the same pattern every time, no \"final_v2_real\".\n  ↳ https://www.diversion.dev/blog/how-naming-conventions-can-boost-your-game-dev-workflow\n- **Never reuse a picture's address for a new version.** A redo is a new /cdn/ file (a /cdn/ name keeps its first picture forever); the old one stays, so before and after both show.\n- **Before/after pairs** for a fix or a second round: file both, same camera, same hour, titled `· before` and `· after` (e.g. \"Toadstool stool · round 1 in game\" and \"· round 2 in game\"), so the creator compares them side by side.\n- **Where they land:** each picture goes into `plan.shots` grouped under the asset id (Gallery › group by asset), with its card and Loom page attached. The creator's own camera shots are `photo` and stay in **Photos**; receipt pictures never go there unless their kind is `photo`.\n- **Same picture twice is one tile.** Filing matches by the /cdn/ address, so syncing again never duplicates.\n- **Check the count:** after syncing, count the asset's Gallery pictures by kind and say it (\"6 renders, 1 concept, 2 drawings, 1 output\").\n\nReference: what each kind holds.\n\n| kind | what it is | made by |\n|---|---|---|\n| `photo` | a Slate camera shot in the game | the creator or a player |\n| `concept` | painted concept art | spawn paint |\n| `drawing` | design sheets, turnarounds, detail sheets, sketches | spawn paint / Blender |\n| `reference` | outside research pictures | the web, saved to /cdn/ |\n| `render` | proof renders, silhouette compare sheets, bakes | Blender, the gate scorer |\n| `output` | the finished asset in the game: engine look, placed model | look |\n\nShot shape: `{ id, url, at, by, kind, asset, title, card, loom, place }`, where `asset` is the receipt id so every picture of one thing groups together. A picture already in the Gallery (same url) is updated, never added twice.\n\n## 4. When another workflow calls\n- **→ Slate log: start**: write the receipt with `status: \"started\"`, the brief and the pipeline, then sync: the ticket appears in `doing`, the Loom page with the brief.\n- **→ Slate log: concept**: `design.concept` on the receipt: the Gallery (`concept`) and the page's picture.\n- **→ Slate log: sheets**: turnarounds and detail sheets (`drawing`), research pictures (`reference`), research links with `usedFor`.\n- **→ Slate log: proof**: renders and gate sheets (`render`), each step's result in `steps` (\"pass\", \"fail: why\", \"skipped: why\").\n- **→ Slate log: landed**: the in-game look (`placement.look`, kind `output`), `limits`, `review`, `status: \"landed\"`; the ticket moves to `test`; add the diary line.\n\n## 5. Check\n- Read the records back after writing: the ticket's column and note, the page's links, the Gallery count by kind. Say what was filed in one line.\n- Open the asset in Workbench and check every row it should have is lit: no \"not yet\" on a step the receipt says passed.\n- Run `slateGate(ctx, \"<id>\")` and quote its `missing` list as-is when it fails.\n\n## 6. Refresh Rules and Journal\n- After filing, update `docs/rules.md` (any new rule the creator gave, in their words) and `docs/journal.md` (move threads between Open, Built and Roadmap; newest state only).\n- The creator saying \"sync journal\" runs this step alone.\n- Then ↻ sync Workbench so Docs shows the new copies.\n\n## 7. Closing gates (every workflow)\n- **Engine limits:** measure the piece against Kiln's Engine limits page (sizes, formats, file size, budgets) and write `limits: { ok, note }` on the receipt with the numbers. Over a limit: stop and say so; only the creator can waive it.\n- **Fresh-eyes review:** someone who didn't make it (a review wisp, or Savi) looks at it in the game, or listens in place, and writes `review: { pass, by, note }`, worst fault first. Fix and look again until it passes.\n- **In-game proof:** a look in the game at the place's real hour (`placement.look`), or for a sound, it playing from its real source in the game.\n- **Gate: Slate filed.** `slateGate(ctx, id)` passes: Workbench, Planner, Loom and Gallery all show the piece.\n"}
];
export const KILN_LIMITS="# Kiln: engine limits\n\nSpawn draws in a browser, on desktops and on phones, and streams every place with no loading screen. Every Kiln workflow reads this page first; a step that wants more than this stops and says so instead of making it. A game can tighten these in its own copy ().\n\n## Everywhere\n- **Phones are the floor.** If it doesn't hold on a phone, it doesn't ship.\n- **No loading screens.** Nothing waits on an asset: the first picture of a place loads about 450 KB, the rest streams in after arrival, so big things are spawned after the player is standing.\n- **One of a kind, many uses.** One file per kind of thing; colour, size and variety come from tint, scale and code, not a new file per variant.\n- **Names are forever.** A /cdn/ name keeps its first result; a change gets a new name (-2, -v3).\n\n## 3D models (.glb)\n- **Texture size:** 1024² by default, 2048² for a hero seen up close, never 4096². A 4K map is about 85 MB of GPU memory with mipmaps, and many phones cap at 4096.\n- **Maps:** base colour, normal, ORM, emissive if it glows; sheen or transmission only on a hero. Every map is download and memory.\n- **Map formats:** JPEG for base colour; PNG for normal and ORM (lossless, so they don't block up in light).\n- **Materials per model:** one where it can be, 2–3 on a hero, slots named for the real surface (velvet_teal, brass_brushed). Each slot is a draw; names let the game tint and swap.\n- **Shaders:** standard PBR material only. Pointiness, Bevel node, AO, procedural noise and node tricks are Cycles-only: bake them or model them, because the export drops them silently.\n- **File size:** a hero under ~1.2 MB, a small prop under ~300 KB, so places stream.\n- **Compression:** Draco, Meshopt and KTX2 are not listed as supported: don't rely on them. An unreadable file is an invisible prop.\n- **Triangles:** big hero 5–8k · mid prop 1.5–3k · small 300–1.5k · character 10–20k; about 500k for everything in frame, for phones.\n- **Rigs:** Mixamo-style bone names for humanoids (the engine's IK, grounding and library clips read them); props move by named nodes, no bones; few shape keys.\n- **Scale and axes:** metres, scale applied, origin at the feet, front −Y in Blender (+Z in glTF). The engine places it as measured.\n- **Repeats:** many copies of one model draw as one batch; distance tiers (visibleRange) and LODs are the engine's job, so don't build your own LOD system.\n\n## Engine-made geometry (scripted primitives)\n- One script + params + seed is one mesh, shared by every copy. Detail by `ctx.lod` (1 full, 4–5 a few dozen faces).\n- ≤ 128 bones in a scripted skeleton.\n- A crowd of tiny things (fish, leaves, birds) is one instanced mesh moved by one manager, never dozens of rigs.\n\n## Textures and pictures\n- Seamless surfaces: named `cdn/texture-<what>.png`, tiled by `repeat` per metre, tinted by colour instead of a new file per colour.\n- Painted pictures (icons, posters, portraits): one set = one style sentence; icons readable at 32 px, transparent when they sit on UI.\n- Texture scripts (`scripts/tex-*.js`) for anything the game computes; keep canvases small (≤ 512² unless it's a sheet).\n\n## 2D sprites\n- Drawn in code by default (crisp, tintable, no download); painted only when finished art is the ask.\n- One sheet per character, a fixed cell grid, frames read with uvRect. 2–4 idle frames, 4–6 walk.\n- Pixel art stays at whole-number scale so it doesn't blur.\n\n## Sound, music, voices\n- MP3 by name: `/cdn/moodboard-<family>/sfx-…`, `music-…`; voices through `spawn voice`, one voice per character.\n- One sound per kind of act; pitch and volume make the variety.\n- Ambient beds are a whisper (gain ≈ 0.15) with a sensible maxDistance; music is one jukebox per place.\n- Sounds answer in the same beat as the act; nothing stacks into noise.\n\n## UI\n- The HUD fits a phone in portrait and landscape; touch targets a thumb wide.\n- The right-middle edge of the screen is Spawn's rail: keep game UI off it.\n- No screen that traps a player with no way out.\n\n## Closing gates (every workflow)\n- **Engine limits:** measure the piece against Kiln's Engine limits page (sizes, formats, file size, budgets) and write `limits: { ok, note }` on the receipt with the numbers. Over a limit: stop and say so; only the creator can waive it.\n- **Fresh-eyes review:** someone who didn't make it (a review wisp, or Savi) looks at it in the game, or listens in place, and writes `review: { pass, by, note }`, worst fault first. Fix and look again until it passes.\n- **In-game proof:** a look in the game at the place's real hour (`placement.look`), or for a sound, it playing from its real source in the game.\n- **Gate: Slate filed.** `slateGate(ctx, id)` passes: Workbench, Planner, Loom and Gallery all show the piece.\n\n- **Concept match (every piece with a concept):** the reviewer, never the builder, renders or captures the piece at the concept's own angle and puts them side by side. They list the concept's defining features, at least five, the things a person would name first (for the toadstool: parallel cable braids across the cushion, a trunk about half the cap's width, four heavy splayed roots, carved facets on the cap, big recessed spots, a knot on a root, moss in the crotches). Each one is ticked `match: true` only if it reads in that render. One miss fails the gate. \"Reads as a toadstool\" is not a match. Write `conceptMatch: { by, render, features: [{ name, match, note }] }`.\n\nHow the gate reads them: `limits.ok` must be `true`, or `limits.waived` holds the creator's own words (only the creator waives; Savi and wisps never write it). `limits.ok: false` fails as \"engine limits not met: <note>\". Any step on the receipt that reads `fail` blocks the gate until it is re-run and passes. A later step never covers for an earlier failure. `review.pass` must be `true` with `review.by` named. With a concept on the receipt, `conceptMatch` must name its reviewer and side-by-side render, list five or more features, and every feature must match. A sound (music, sound effects, voices) proves itself with an `audio` file on the receipt plus `placement` (its source in the game); everything else with `placement.look`.\n\n### What the fresh-eyes reviewer looks for, by kind\nWorst fault first in `review.note`; one fault per line.\n- **Model:** reads as the brief at play distance (1.5–3 m) in the place's real light; silhouette matches the concept; feet on the ground, no float or sink; scale against a door (2 m) or a chair seat (0.45 m); no black faces, stretched UVs, seams or shading blocks; materials read as the real surface; the collider fits what you see.\n- **Texture:** no visible seam or repeat pattern at the tiling it ships with; texel density matches its neighbours; no blocky normal map in raking light; colour sits in the palette and reads in greyscale.\n- **Picture (icon, poster, portrait):** reads at the size it's shown (an icon at 32 px); one style with its set; no garbled text or extra fingers; transparent edge clean on UI.\n- **Sprite:** whole-pixel scale, no blur or half-pixel shimmer; frames line up on the cell grid (no jitter); feet on the same baseline every frame; loop has no pop.\n- **Music:** the loop point is seamless; level sits under the game's sounds; mood matches the place's hour; no clipping or a dead silent tail.\n- **Sound effect:** lands on the same beat as the act; no click at the start or end; not louder than its neighbours; repeated ten times it doesn't grate (pitch varies).\n- **Voice:** the character's own voice every line; words clear without subtitles; no breaths, clicks or room hiss; level matches the other lines.\n\n---\nUnknowns go to the Spawn team as a question, not into the game as a guess.\n";
